TP.register({id:"lp6-g8",lp:6,grade:"8",mode:"curriculum",checks:5,nextGrade:"high school",build(E){const F=E.F;const ico=(n,s=72)=>F.icon(n,s);const STORY_TITLE="The Switch";const STORY=`<p>The town gave the handle to the Obengs because Elias Obeng had strung the Alder Street line in 1961 and kept it lit for forty-one years, and because somebody on the council thought it would make a good photograph.</p>
<p>Nadia was fourteen and had been told to be at the substation at ten minutes to nine. Her grandfather had been told the same thing and had not said yes.</p>
<p>“It is not a funeral,” she told him on Tuesday. He was at the kitchen table with the paper folded to the crossword and a pen he was not using.</p>
<p>“No,” he said. “It is an inventory.”</p>
<p>She did not know what to do with that, so she left it alone for two days.</p>
<p>On Thursday she tried again, and this time she brought the thing she had been saving. “There are a hundred and eighteen stars over Riverside Park now,” she said. “There were forty-one. They counted.”</p>
<p>“I know what they counted.”</p>
<p>“You could see them.”</p>
<p>“I have seen stars.” He turned the paper over. “I wired that line when there was nothing on this street at six o’clock but one bulb at the mill gate and whatever the moon was doing. People could see the sky fine. They wanted the street.”</p>
<p>Nadia thought about that for the rest of the week. It had not occurred to her that the dark sky was the thing her grandfather had been hired to take away, or that forty-one years of good work was exactly what the town was now undoing, carefully, with a grant.</p>
<p>At ten minutes to nine on the first clear night of January she was at the substation with four councilors, a photographer, and a handle the size of her forearm.</p>
<p>Her grandfather came at five of. He did not look at the camera. He stood where he could see the street and put one hand flat on the housing of the old cabinet, the way you would put a hand on a horse.</p>
<p>“Go on,” he said.</p>
<p>She pulled it, and twelve blocks of mercury light went out at once, and for about four seconds Alder Street was darker than she had ever seen a street, and then the new lamps came up, low and white, pointing at the pavement.</p>
<p>Her grandfather looked up the street. Then he looked up.</p>
<p>“Well,” he said. “Look at that.”</p>
<p>Nadia could not tell which one he meant, and she has never asked.</p>`;const story=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${STORY_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${STORY}</div>`;const SKY_TITLE="How a Town Gets Its Sky Back";const SKYTEXT=`<p>A street lamp does two jobs at once, and only one of them is wanted. The wanted job is to put light on the pavement. The unwanted one is to put light into the air, where dust and water droplets scatter it back down as a dim, even glow. That glow, called skyglow, is what erases faint stars. A star does not get dimmer. It stops standing out against a brighter background.</p>
<p>Skyglow is measured, not guessed at. A sky quality meter is pointed straight up and reads the brightness of one patch of sky in magnitudes per square arcsecond, a scale on which a larger number means a darker sky. A reading of 18 is a bright suburban sky. A reading of 21.5 is a rural sky with the Milky Way plainly visible. The scale is not linear: each step of one magnitude is a change of about two and a half times in brightness, so the gap between 20 and 21 is wider than it looks.</p>
<p>Two things make a lamp a skyglow problem: where it points and what color it is. A fixture with no cap sends ten to thirty percent of its output above the horizontal. Color matters because short-wavelength blue light scatters in the air far more than longer-wavelength amber light does, which is why a cool white lamp brightens the sky more than a warm one of the same output.</p>
<p>Hollowell replaced 420 street lamps over two years with capped, amber-leaning fixtures aimed at the pavement. Readings taken from the same fixed post at the edge of town rose from 19.1 to 19.9, which is a sky about twice as dark. The street lighting bill fell about twenty percent. The number of stars a volunteer could count from Riverside Park went from 41 to 118.</p>
<p>None of that makes the sky over Hollowell a rural sky. A reading of 19.9 is still well short of 21.5, and the town is still ringed by lights it does not own: a highway interchange, a distribution warehouse that runs its lot lights all night, and the glow of a city thirty miles east that no local ordinance can reach.</p>
<p>What the project shows is narrower and more useful than a rescue. It shows that the part of a town’s skyglow the town itself makes is the part a town can take back, and that taking it back costs less over twenty years than leaving it alone.</p>`;const skyText=()=>`<h3 style="margin:0 0 6px;color:var(--sea)">${SKY_TITLE}</h3><div class="passage sm">${SKYTEXT}</div>`;const MEMO_TITLE="Put the Money into the Warehouse Lot";const MEMO_SRC="Memo from Councilor Dmitri Vasquez to the Hollowell town council. March 2026.";const MEMO=`<p>The $75,000 left in the lighting fund should go to shielding the lights on the Cobb Road distribution warehouse lot, not to the sixty remaining lamps on the river road. Three reasons follow, and then one point from another town.</p>
<p>First, output. The warehouse lot runs 88 unshielded floodlights all night. A sky reading taken 200 meters north of the lot is 18.3, against a town average of 19.9. On the magnitude scale that is a difference of 1.6, which is a sky roughly four times brighter. The river road, by comparison, runs through a stretch where the reading is already 19.6.</p>
<p>Second, cost. The warehouse owner has offered to pay half of the $84,000 shielding job if the town pays the rest, so our $42,000 buys 88 fixtures. The river road job is $71,000 for sixty.</p>
<p>Third, opinion. I asked the eleven people at the Cobb Road neighborhood meeting whether the warehouse lights bothered them, and all eleven said yes.</p>
<p>One more point. Brayford shielded its warehouse district two years ago and its downtown vacancy rate fell, which shows that dark-sky work brings businesses in.</p>
<p>Also worth noting: a survey last year found that 71 percent of Hollowell residents describe the lantern walk as their favorite town event.</p>`;const memoText=()=>`<article><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${MEMO_TITLE}</h3><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 8px">${MEMO_SRC}</p><div class="passage sm">${MEMO}</div></article>`;const X_SRC="Text X: Letter to the Hollowell town council from Imani Boateng, operations manager, Cobb Road Distribution Center. March 2026.";const X_TEXT=`<p>Our lot carries 64 unshielded floodlights, not 88. Twenty-four of the fixtures Councilor Vasquez counted are on the loading dock canopy, and those are already capped and aimed down.</p>
<p>We also dispute the reading. Our own contractor took a reading 200 meters north of the lot on February 9 and got 18.9, not 18.3.</p>
<p>We stand by our offer to match the town dollar for dollar. But the lot is not the town’s largest source of skyglow. The highway interchange two miles west is, and everyone involved knows it. The town should spend its $75,000 on the river road, where it owns every lamp and controls every decision. Our lot is on a replacement schedule and will be fully shielded by 2029 at our own expense.</p>`;const Y_SRC="Text Y: Note to the Hollowell town council from the Night Sky Monitoring Project. March 2026.";const Y_TEXT=`<p>We take readings from a fixed post 200 meters north of the Cobb Road lot on the first clear, moonless night of every month. Our February reading was 18.3. A reading of 18.9 is possible at that post on a night with thin high cloud, which scatters more light downward in some conditions and less in others, or on a night when the lot’s two east bays are not running.</p>
<p>On the fixture count: we walked the parcel on February 11 and found 88 fixtures. Nine of the twenty-four canopy fixtures carried an intact cap. The other fifteen had the cap removed or broken, so they were running unshielded on the night of our reading.</p>
<p>We agree that the highway interchange puts more light into the sky than the lot does. We do not agree that this makes the lot the wrong target. The interchange is lit on the state’s budget and no town vote can change it. The lot can be changed this year with the owner paying half. “Largest source” is not the question in front of the council. “Largest source the council can act on” is.</p>`;const SRC_A_CARD="Report: “Glare, Output, and Obstacle Detection: A Field Study of 1,100 Trials.” By Dr. Hannah Ozturk, lighting engineer, State Transportation Research Office, 2025.";const SRC_A_TEXT="Walkers detected a low obstacle at 20 meters 18 percent more often under shielded fixtures than under unshielded fixtures of the same output. Glare, not total output, was the strongest single predictor of a missed obstacle.";const SOURCES=()=>`<p class="big" style="font-size:1.55rem">Research question: Does switching a street to shielded lamps make the sidewalk less safe to walk on?</p><div style="display:flex;gap:16px;flex-wrap:wrap;margin-bottom:14px">${srcCard("Source A",SRC_A_CARD,SRC_A_TEXT)}${srcCard("Source B","A post on a home improvement forum by a user named corner_lot_dan. No date.","I read somewhere that capped streetlights put out half the light. Pretty sure that is just physics. Seems like a bad trade for saving some stars.")}</div>`;const checklist=items=>`<ul class="qlist" style="margin-top:14px">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;const factBox=(title,items)=>`<div style="border:3px dashed var(--line);border-radius:16px;background:var(--surface);padding:10px 20px;margin:14px 0"><p class="fig-label" style="margin:0 0 6px;text-align:left">${title}</p><ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2em;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${items.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul></div>`;const bigTable=(head,rows,cap)=>`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="chart" style="margin:14px auto;font-size:1.3rem">${cap?`<caption style="font-size:1.1rem;padding-bottom:6px">${cap}</caption>`:""}<tr>${head.map(h=>`<th style="text-align:center">${h}</th>`).join("")}</tr>${rows.map(r=>`<tr>${r.map((c,i)=>i?`<td style="text-align:center">${c}</td>`:`<th>${c}</th>`).join("")}</tr>`).join("")}</table></div>`;function srcCard(tag,head,body){return`<article style="flex:1 1 320px;border:2px solid var(--line);border-radius:16px;padding:12px 16px;background:var(--surface)"><h3 style="margin:0 0 4px;color:var(--sea)">${tag}</h3><p style="margin:0 0 8px;font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700">${head}</p><p style="margin:0;font-size:1.25rem;line-height:1.5">${body}</p></article>`}const twoTexts=()=>`<style>body:not(.sup-big) .texts article p{font-size:1.06rem;line-height:1.5}</style><div class="texts"><article><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${X_SRC}</p>${X_TEXT}</article><article><p class="src" style="margin:0 0 6px">${Y_SRC}</p>${Y_TEXT}</article></div>`;const lead=t=>`<p class="big">${t}</p>`;const LAMP_RATE=[[0,0],[2,30],[4,60],[6,90],[8,120]];const SCATTER=[[.5,18],[1,26],[1.5,33],[2,45],[2.5,54],[3,68],[3.5,76],[2.2,120]];function plot(o){const m={l:o.yl?74:38,r:26,t:o.title?34:20,b:o.xl?54:30},sx=o.sx,sy=o.sy,W=o.x1*sx,H=o.y1*sy;const X=x=>m.l+x*sx,Y=y=>m.t+(o.y1-y)*sy,gx=o.gx||1,gy=o.gy||1,lx=o.lx||gx,ly=o.ly||gy;let s=`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${W+m.l+m.r} ${H+m.t+m.b}" width="${o.w||W+m.l+m.r}" role="img" aria-label="${o.alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">`;if(o.title)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="21" font-size="17" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.title}</text>`;s+=`<rect x="${m.l}" y="${m.t}" width="${W}" height="${H}" fill="var(--surface)"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=gx)s+=`<line x1="${X(x)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(x)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=gy)s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(y)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(y)}" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1"/>`;s+=`<line x1="${m.l}" y1="${Y(0)}" x2="${m.l+W}" y2="${Y(0)}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/><line x1="${X(0)}" y1="${m.t}" x2="${X(0)}" y2="${m.t+H}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>`;for(let x=0;x<=o.x1+1e-9;x+=lx)s+=`<text x="${X(x)}" y="${Y(0)+18}" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${+x.toFixed(3)}</text>`;for(let y=0;y<=o.y1+1e-9;y+=ly)s+=`<text x="${m.l-7}" y="${Y(y)}" font-size="13" text-anchor="end" dominant-baseline="central" fill="var(--ink)">${+y.toFixed(3)}</text>`;if(o.xl)s+=`<text x="${m.l+W/2}" y="${m.t+H+46}" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.xl}</text>`;if(o.yl)s+=`<text transform="translate(20,${m.t+H/2}) rotate(-90)" font-size="15" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" fill="var(--ink)">${o.yl}</text>`;(o.paths||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<polyline points="${p.pts.map(q=>X(q[0])+","+Y(q[1])).join(" ")}" fill="none" stroke="${p.c||"var(--sea)"}" stroke-width="3.5" stroke-linejoin="round" ${p.dash?'stroke-dasharray="9 6"':""}/>`});(o.pts||[]).forEach(p=>{s+=`<circle cx="${X(p[0])}" cy="${Y(p[1])}" r="6" fill="${p[2]||"var(--coral)"}" stroke="var(--ink)" stroke-width="1.4"/>`});(o.labels||[]).forEach(l=>{s+=`<text x="${X(l[0])}" y="${Y(l[1])}" font-size="16" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" text-anchor="${l[2]||"start"}" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="4">${l[3]}</text>`});return s+"</svg>"}const crewGraph=()=>plot({alt:"Graph of blocks from the bridge against minutes after seven o'clock. Crew One rises in a straight line from the origin. Crew Two falls in a dashed straight line from 12 blocks at zero minutes down to 6 blocks at 24 minutes. The two lines cross.",title:"Two crews on the lantern line",x1:24,y1:12,sx:19,sy:19,gx:4,lx:4,gy:2,ly:2,xl:"Minutes after seven o’clock",yl:"Blocks from the bridge",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[24,12]]},{pts:[[0,12],[24,6]],c:"var(--coral)",dash:true}],labels:[[6.5,2.3,"start","Crew One"],[1,10.2,"start","Crew Two"]]});const pointGraph=()=>plot({alt:"Graph with five plotted points: 1 comma 2, 2 comma 5, 4 comma 6, 4 comma 10, and 6 comma 9.",title:"Graph D",x1:8,y1:12,sx:34,sy:19,gx:1,lx:1,gy:2,ly:2,xl:"Input",yl:"Output",pts:[[1,2],[2,5],[4,6],[4,10],[6,9]]});const rateGraph=()=>plot({alt:"Graph of lamps replaced against months. A straight line rises from the origin through the points 2 comma 30, 4 comma 60, 6 comma 90, and 8 comma 120.",title:"Crew A: lamps replaced",x1:8,y1:120,sx:44,sy:1.7,gx:1,lx:2,gy:15,ly:30,xl:"Months on the job",yl:"Lamps replaced",paths:[{pts:[[0,0],[8,120]]}],pts:LAMP_RATE});const scatterFig=()=>plot({alt:"Scatter plot of stars counted against kilometres from the middle of town. Eight points. Seven of them rise steadily from the point 0.5 comma 18 up to 3.5 comma 76. One point at 2.2 comma 120 sits far above the others.",title:"Stars counted, by distance from the middle of town",x1:4,y1:130,sx:92,sy:1.55,gx:.5,lx:1,gy:20,ly:20,xl:"Kilometres from the middle of town",yl:"Stars counted by eye",pts:SCATTER});const panelGrid=()=>F.coordGrid({max:6,quad4:true,polys:[[[1,1],[4,1],[1,3]]],size:340});const tableA=()=>bigTable(["Minutes after seven","0","5","10","15"],[["Lanterns lit","0","9","18","27"]],"Table A");const tableB=()=>bigTable(["Block number","1","2","3","4"],[["Lamps on that block","6","6","7","6"]],"Table B");const tableC=()=>bigTable(["Price of a paper lantern","$3","$5","$3","$6"],[["Number sold that night","40","22","35","14"]],"Table C");const brightTable=()=>bigTable(["Distance from the lamp (m)","1","2","3","4"],[["How bright it looks (units)","144","36","16","9"]],"One lamp, measured at four distances");const T=(x,y,t,a,fs)=>`<text x="${x}" y="${y}" text-anchor="${a||"middle"}" font-size="${fs||16}" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)" paint-order="stroke" stroke="var(--surface)" stroke-width="3.5">${t}</text>`;const svg=(w,h,width,alt,inner)=>`<svg viewBox="0 0 ${w} ${h}" width="${width}" role="img" aria-label="${alt}" style="max-width:100%;height:auto">${inner}</svg>`;const poleFig=()=>svg(240,230,250,"A vertical lamp post labeled 24 feet, a ground line, and a straight guy wire from the top of the post to a stake 10 feet from the base. The wire is labeled with a question mark and there is a right angle mark at the base.",`<line x1="56" y1="196" x2="206" y2="196" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/>
   <line x1="80" y1="196" x2="80" y2="30" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="5"/>
   <line x1="80" y1="30" x2="149" y2="196" stroke="var(--sea)" stroke-width="3.5"/>
   <path d="M92 196 v-12 h-12" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
   <circle cx="149" cy="196" r="5" fill="var(--coral)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/>
   ${T(70,116,"24 ft","end")}${T(115,218,"10 ft")}${T(126,98,"? ft","start")}`);const crateFig=()=>svg(330,210,330,"A box drawn in three dimensions with edges labeled 12 inches long, 9 inches deep, and 8 inches high, and a dashed line drawn inside it from one bottom corner to the opposite top corner.",`<polygon points="50,70 206,70 254,34 98,34" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <polygon points="206,70 254,34 254,138 206,174" fill="var(--gold)" fill-opacity=".4" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <rect x="50" y="70" width="156" height="104" fill="var(--sea-soft)" fill-opacity=".6" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2.5"/>
   <line x1="50" y1="174" x2="254" y2="34" stroke="var(--coral)" stroke-width="2.6" stroke-dasharray="7 5"/>
   ${T(128,198,"12 in")}${T(44,126,"8 in","end")}${T(88,52,"9 in","start")}${T(168,128,"?","start",20)}`);const gridFig=()=>F.coordGrid({max:10,quad4:true,size:360,points:[[-3,2,"A"],[5,8,"B"]],lines:[[[-3,2],[5,8]]]});const STOPS=[{id:1,title:"The Switch",short:"The Switch",parts:[{std:["RL.8.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence for what a story says and for inferences",html:story,qs:["Why did the town give the handle to the Obeng family? Read me the exact words that prove it.","Which one is the strongest evidence that Elias does not think the old lamps were a mistake? Pick A, B, or C and tell me why. A: “It is an inventory.” B: “I wired that line when there was nothing on this street at six o’clock but one bulb at the mill gate and whatever the moon was doing. People could see the sky fine. They wanted the street.” C: “He did not look at the camera.”","Nadia says the night is not a funeral and her grandfather answers, “It is an inventory.” What does he mean? Read me the words that most strongly support your answer.","At the end, “Nadia could not tell which one he meant.” What are the two things he could have meant? Read me the words that most strongly support each one."],key:["Because Elias Obeng built and maintained the line, and because a councilor wanted a photograph. Reads “because Elias Obeng had strung the Alder Street line in 1961 and kept it lit for forty-one years, and because somebody on the council thought it would make a good photograph.” (RL.8.1, explicit evidence)","B. It gives his reason: the town already had a dark sky and chose the street light instead, so his line did what people asked for. A shows he will not treat the night as a tribute but does not say the lamps were right, and C shows only that he dislikes the ceremony. (RL.8.1, strongest evidence)","He means the night is a counting up of what he built and what it is now worth, not a mourning of it: he expects to be measured, not honored. Strongest support: “I wired that line when there was nothing on this street at six o’clock… They wanted the street,” and Nadia’s realization that “forty-one years of good work was exactly what the town was now undoing, carefully, with a grant.” (RL.8.1, inference with the strongest support)","He could have meant the new lamps on the street, or the stars above it. “Her grandfather looked up the street. Then he looked up” supplies both directions. For the street: “the new lamps came up, low and white, pointing at the pavement.” For the sky: “There are a hundred and eighteen stars over Riverside Park now… There were forty-one.” (RL.8.1, two supported readings)"]}]},{id:2,title:"Two Crews, One Line",short:"Two Crews",parts:[{std:["8.EE.C.8"],dur:240,dir:"Use the graph for number 1. Solve the rest on your paper and hold it up. Check each answer in both equations.",desc:"solving pairs of simultaneous linear equations by graph and by algebra, including word problems and the number of solutions",html:()=>lead("Two crews hang the lantern line on the same twelve blocks. Crew One starts at the bridge at seven o’clock and works east. Crew Two starts twelve blocks out and works back toward the bridge."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${crewGraph()}</div>`,qs:["At what point do the two lines cross? What does that point mean for the two crews?","Solve this pair with algebra: y = 3x − 4 and y = −2x + 11.","Solve this pair with algebra: x + y = 47 and x − y = 9.","Without solving all the way, tell how many solutions each pair has and how you know. (a) y = −4x + 6 and y = −4x − 1. (b) 2x − 5y = 10 and 6x − 15y = 30.","A crate holds 30 lanterns in all, some paper and some glass. A paper lantern weighs 0.4 kg and a glass lantern weighs 1.2 kg. The lanterns in the crate weigh 20 kg altogether. Write a pair of equations and find how many of each.","Crew One starts hanging at 7:00 and hangs 8 lanterns an hour. Crew Three starts at 8:00 on the same line and hangs 12 an hour. Write a pair of equations and find when Crew Three has hung as many lanterns as Crew One, and how many that is."],key:["(16, 8). Sixteen minutes after seven o’clock, both crews are 8 blocks from the bridge, so that is when and where they meet on the line. Partial: the crossing point read right with no account of what it means is approaching. (8.EE.C.8a)","x = 3 and y = 5, or (3, 5). Steps: 3x − 4 = −2x + 11, so 5x = 15 and x = 3, then y = 3(3) − 4 = 5. Check in the other equation: −2(3) + 11 = 5. Or: substitution, elimination, or graphing both lines all count. Partial: x = 3 with no y is approaching. (8.EE.C.8b)","x = 28 and y = 19, or (28, 19). Steps: add the two equations to get 2x = 56, so x = 28, then 28 + y = 47 gives y = 19. Check: 28 − 19 = 9. Or: substitution, y = 47 − x into x − y = 9, reaches the same pair. Partial: x = 28 with no y is approaching. (8.EE.C.8b)","(a) No solution. Both slopes are −4 and the y-intercepts differ, so the lines are parallel and never cross. Substituting gives 6 = −1, which is never true. (b) Infinitely many solutions. Multiplying the first equation by 3 gives 6x − 15y = 30, which is the second equation, so they are the same line. For example (5, 0) works in both. Partial: one of the two pairs answered is approaching. (8.EE.C.8b)","20 paper lanterns and 10 glass lanterns. Equations: p + g = 30 and 0.4p + 1.2g = 20. Steps: p = 30 − g, so 0.4(30 − g) + 1.2g = 20, which gives 12 + 0.8g = 20, so 0.8g = 8 and g = 10, then p = 20. Check: 20 + 10 = 30 and 0.4(20) + 1.2(10) = 8 + 12 = 20. Or: elimination after clearing the decimals, or guess and check against both conditions. Partial: a correct pair of equations with the solving unfinished is approaching. (8.EE.C.8c)","At 10:00, with 24 lanterns each. With t as hours after 7:00: n = 8t and n = 12(t − 1). Steps: 8t = 12t − 12, so 4t = 12 and t = 3, which is 10:00, and n = 8(3) = 24. Check: 12(3 − 1) = 24. Or: build a table of the two counts hour by hour and look for the row where they match. Partial: the equations right with the time or the count missing is approaching. (8.EE.C.8c)"]}]},{id:3,title:"The Line That Turns It",short:"Turning Line",parts:[{std:["RL.8.3"],dur:140,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the line or the sentence you are talking about.",desc:"explaining how lines of dialogue and incidents propel the action, reveal character, and provoke a decision",html:story,qs:["Elias answers Nadia with one line: “It is an inventory.” How does that line change the moment, and what does it reveal about him?","On Thursday Nadia brings him the star counts. How does that incident push the story forward? What does his answer do to her?","What decision does the Thursday conversation provoke in Nadia? Where in the story do you see it?","“He stood where he could see the street and put one hand flat on the housing of the old cabinet.” What does that action reveal about him, and how does it prepare the ending?"],key:["It refuses her framing and ends the argument on the spot: “She did not know what to do with that, so she left it alone for two days.” It reveals that he expects the night to be an accounting of his work rather than a tribute to it, and that he will not be talked into a role somebody else has written for him. (RL.8.3, dialogue propels the action and reveals character)","It is her strongest argument, the one “she had been saving,” and it fails. “I know what they counted” tells her the numbers were never the obstacle. That moves the story out of persuading him and into understanding him, which is what the next paragraph does: she sees that the dark sky “was the thing her grandfather had been hired to take away.” (RL.8.3, an incident propels the action)","She stops arguing. She “thought about that for the rest of the week,” and at the substation she makes no further case. When he says “Go on,” she pulls the handle. The decision is to let him come, or not come, on his own terms rather than hers. (RL.8.3, dialogue provokes a decision)","He came for the line and not for the ceremony: he turns away from the camera, positions himself to watch the street, and touches the equipment he built. The comparison, “the way you would put a hand on a horse,” makes it a goodbye to something he worked alongside. It prepares the ending because he is already facing the street, so when the new lamps come up he looks up the street first and only then looks up at the sky. (RL.8.3, an incident reveals character)"]}]},{id:4,title:"Word by Word",short:"Word Choice",parts:[{std:["RL.8.4"],dur:135,dir:"Read the story to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the words you are talking about.",desc:"the meaning of words and phrases, including connotation, figurative language, and the effect of specific word choices",html:story,qs:["Nadia says the night is “not a funeral.” Her grandfather answers, “It is an inventory.” Compare what those two words suggest. Why does inventory fit what he thinks is happening?","“He put one hand flat on the housing of the old cabinet, the way you would put a hand on a horse.” What kind of figurative language is that? What does it tell you about how he sees the equipment?","“For about four seconds Alder Street was darker than she had ever seen a street.” Why does the author count the seconds instead of writing “for a moment”? What is the effect?","The new lamps come up “low and white.” What do those two words suggest about the new light, and how do they work against “twelve blocks of mercury light”?"],key:["Funeral carries grief, ceremony, and honoring something that is gone. Inventory carries counting, stock, and deciding what a thing is still worth. He picks inventory because he thinks the town is totting up his forty-one years and writing part of it off, which is a cold accounting rather than a tribute, and because it refuses the ceremony the council has planned. (RL.8.4, connotative meaning)","A simile, signaled by “the way you would.” It turns the cabinet into something living that he has worked alongside and is steadying or quieting, so the old line reads as a working animal being retired rather than a machine being scrapped. It also shows he is saying goodbye to the equipment and not to the crowd. (RL.8.4, figurative language)","Four seconds is short, but counting it makes the reader sit in it instead of passing over it, and a measured number fits the inventory idea that runs through the scene. “A moment” would be vague and would slide by. The exact count also marks the only piece of the night that belongs to neither the old lamps nor the new ones. (RL.8.4, the effect of a specific word choice)","Low says the light is aimed down and kept near the ground. White says it is plain and cool rather than the orange cast of the old mercury lamps. Together they describe a set of small, aimed pools. “Twelve blocks of mercury light” is one wide sheet of colored light going out all at once, so the contrast is between a wash over everything and light placed where it is wanted. (RL.8.4, analyzing word choice)"]}]},{id:5,title:"One In, One Out",short:"Functions",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.1"],dur:165,dir:"Use the three tables and Graph D. Answer out loud, and use the definition of a function in every answer.",desc:"understanding a function as a rule that assigns exactly one output to each input, and reading the graph of a function",html:()=>lead("A function is a rule that gives each input exactly one output. These are four sets of records the festival crew kept."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-direction:column;align-items:center;gap:4px">${tableA()}${tableB()}${tableC()}${pointGraph()}</div>`,qs:["Is Table A a function? Is Table B a function? Use the definition in each answer.","Is Table C a function? Explain.","Is the set of points in Graph D a function? How can you tell by looking at the graph?","A rule says: to each lantern on Alder Street, assign the block it hangs on. Is that a function? Now turn it around: to each block, assign the lantern that hangs on it. Is that a function?","The oil left in a lamp follows the rule: each number of hours lit, h, gets 180 − 23h milliliters. Find the output when h = 4 and write the ordered pair. Then describe what the graph of this function looks like."],key:["Table A is a function. Each input, 0, 5, 10, and 15 minutes, appears once and has exactly one output. Table B is also a function. The inputs 1, 2, 3, and 4 each appear once with one output, and the fact that blocks 1, 2, and 4 share the output 6 does not matter, because the definition only forbids one input from having two outputs. Partial: one of the two tables answered is approaching. (8.F.A.1, the definition of a function)","No. The input $3 appears twice, once with 40 and once with 35, so that input does not get exactly one output. Partial: no with no account of the repeated input is approaching. (8.F.A.1)","No. The input 4 has two points, (4, 6) and (4, 10), so one input has two outputs. By eye, a vertical line drawn at 4 would cross the graph twice, and the graph of a function is crossed at most once by any vertical line. Or: naming the vertical line test counts as the by-eye reason. (8.F.A.1, the graph of a function)","The first rule is a function, because each lantern hangs on exactly one block. The second is not, because a block holds more than one lantern, so that input would be assigned several outputs. Partial: one of the two rules answered is approaching. (8.F.A.1, deciding whether a rule is a function)","88 milliliters, because 23 × 4 = 92 and 180 − 92 = 88. The ordered pair is (4, 88). The graph is the set of all ordered pairs (h, 180 − 23h), and because the change is the same for every hour those pairs lie on a straight line that slopes down. Partial: the output and the pair right with nothing said about the graph is approaching. (8.F.A.1, the graph of a function is the set of ordered pairs)"]}]},{id:6,title:"Light and Distance",short:"Linear?",parts:[{std:["8.F.A.3"],dur:180,dir:"Use the table for numbers 3 and 4. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer. Sketch number 2.",desc:"interpreting y = mx + b as a linear function and giving examples of functions that are not linear",html:()=>lead("A lighting crew is paid one way, and a lamp looks brighter or dimmer another way. Only one of those is a linear function."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure">${brightTable()}</div>`,qs:["A crew is paid a $40 call-out plus $26 for each lamp it changes, so the pay is p = 26n + 40. Is that a linear function? Name m and b and say what each one means here.","Sketch the graph of p = 26n + 40 from n = 0 to n = 5. Name two points that are on your line.","Look at the table. Is how bright the lamp looks a linear function of the distance? Work out the changes and explain.","Give an example of a function that is not linear, other than the one in the table, and say how you know it is not linear.","Which of these define linear functions? (a) y = 7 − 5x (b) y = x<sup>2</sup> + 3 (c) y = 4 (d) y = 2 ÷ x (e) 3x + 2y = 12","The number of lanterns still to hang is r = 120 − 8t, where t is minutes. Is it linear? What do the 120 and the −8 mean, and what does the graph look like?"],key:["Yes. It has the form y = mx + b, so its graph is a straight line. m = 26 is the slope, the pay for each extra lamp. b = 40 is the value when n = 0, the call-out the crew is paid before changing any lamp. Partial: yes with one of m and b not explained is approaching. (8.F.A.3, interpreting y = mx + b)","A straight line from (0, 40) up to (5, 170). Two points, such as (0, 40) and (5, 170), or (1, 66), (2, 92), (3, 118), (4, 144). Check: 26 × 5 = 130 and 130 + 40 = 170. Partial: a correct line with only one point named is approaching. (8.F.A.3, the graph of a linear function is a straight line)","No, it is not linear. From 1 m to 2 m the brightness changes by 36 − 144 = −108. From 2 m to 3 m it changes by 16 − 36 = −20. From 3 m to 4 m it changes by 9 − 16 = −7. The distance goes up by the same step each time but the brightness does not change by the same amount, so the rate of change is not constant, the graph curves, and the rule cannot be written as y = mx + b. Or: sketch the four pairs and show they curve. Partial: no with one difference worked out is approaching. (8.F.A.3, recognizing a function that is not linear)","Any correct example with a reason, such as: the area of a square lantern panel, A = s<sup>2</sup>, where s = 1, 2, 3, 4 gives 1, 4, 9, 16 and the differences 3, 5, 7 are not equal, so the graph curves. Also fine: y = 2<sup>x</sup>, y = 1 ÷ x, or the volume of a cube. The student must say why it is not linear, not just name it. (8.F.A.3, giving examples of functions that are not linear)","Linear: (a), with m = −5 and b = 7; (c), with m = 0 and b = 4, which graphs as a horizontal line; and (e), because solving for y gives y = −1.5x + 6. Not linear: (b), because x is squared, and (d), because x is in the denominator. Partial: (c) and (e) are the two usually missed, so three or four of the five sorted right is approaching. (8.F.A.3)","Linear. b = 120 is the number still to hang at t = 0, and m = −8 means 8 fewer each minute, so the line falls as t goes up. The graph is a straight line starting at (0, 120) and reaching 0 at t = 15, because 120 ÷ 8 = 15. Partial: linear with one of the two numbers explained is approaching. (8.F.A.3, interpreting m and b in context)"]}]},{id:7,title:"Reading the Dark",short:"Dark Sky",parts:[{std:["RI.8.1"],dur:140,dir:"Read the article to yourself. Then answer out loud. Read me the exact words that prove each answer.",desc:"citing the strongest evidence from a science article for an explicit answer and for inferences",html:skyText,qs:["On the sky quality meter scale, does a larger number mean a darker sky or a brighter one? Read me the exact words that prove it.","Which single sentence most strongly supports the claim that Hollowell’s new lamps made a real, measured difference to the sky? Pick A, B, or C and tell me why. A: “The street lighting bill fell about twenty percent.” B: “Readings taken from the same fixed post at the edge of town rose from 19.1 to 19.9, which is a sky about twice as dark.” C: “Hollowell replaced 420 street lamps over two years with capped, amber-leaning fixtures aimed at the pavement.”","Claim: the color of a lamp matters, not only its shield. Find the two sentences that together most strongly support this claim and read them to me.","A nearby town with the same number of street lamps replaces all of them the same way and gets a reading of 19.4 instead of 19.9. What is the most likely reason? Read me the words that most strongly support your inference."],key:["A larger number means a darker sky. Reads “a scale on which a larger number means a darker sky.” (RI.8.1, explicit evidence)","B. It is a before-and-after measurement of the sky itself, taken from the same fixed post, and the article has already explained what the scale means. A measures money rather than sky. C describes what the town did, not what resulted from it. (RI.8.1, strongest evidence)","“Two things make a lamp a skyglow problem: where it points and what color it is.” and “Color matters because short-wavelength blue light scatters in the air far more than longer-wavelength amber light does, which is why a cool white lamp brightens the sky more than a warm one of the same output.” Both are needed: the first names color as one of the two causes and the second gives the reason. (RI.8.1, two sentences that together support a claim)","Something it does not own is lighting its sky, such as a highway, a warehouse lot, or a city nearby. Strongest support: “the town is still ringed by lights it does not own: a highway interchange, a distribution warehouse that runs its lot lights all night, and the glow of a city thirty miles east that no local ordinance can reach.” (RI.8.1, inference with the strongest support)"]}]},{id:8,title:"Pole and Diagonal",short:"Pythagoras",parts:[{std:["8.G.B.7"],dur:200,dir:"Use the three pictures. Work on your paper and hold it up. Show the squares you added or subtracted.",desc:"applying the Pythagorean theorem in two and three dimensions, including on the coordinate plane",html:()=>lead("A guy wire is the straight cable that holds a lamp post upright. The crew works out lengths it cannot reach with a tape."),fig:()=>`<div class="figure" style="flex-wrap:wrap;gap:20px;align-items:flex-start"><div style="text-align:center">${poleFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 1</p></div><div style="text-align:center">${crateFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 2</p></div><div style="text-align:center">${gridFig()}<p class="fig-label" style="margin:2px 0 0">Picture 3</p></div></div>`,qs:["Picture 1. The lamp post is 24 ft tall and the stake is 10 ft from its base. How long is the guy wire?","A different guy wire is 41 ft long and runs from the top of a 40 ft post down to the ground. How far from the base of that post is its stake?","Picture 3. Find the distance from point A to point B. Use the grid, not a ruler.","Picture 2. The lantern crate is 12 in long, 9 in deep, and 8 in high. What is the longest straight rod that will fit inside it, corner to corner?","A right triangle has legs of 7 cm and 9 cm. Give the hypotenuse exactly, and then to the nearest tenth of a centimeter.","A 25 ft ladder leans against a wall with its foot 7 ft out from the wall. How far up the wall does the top of the ladder reach?"],key:["26 ft. 24<sup>2</sup> + 10<sup>2</sup> = 576 + 100 = 676, and the square root of 676 is 26. Or: recognise the 5, 12, 13 triple scaled by 2. Partial: 676 found with no square root taken is approaching. (8.G.B.7, finding the hypotenuse)","9 ft. The wire is the hypotenuse, so 41<sup>2</sup> − 40<sup>2</sup> = 1,681 − 1,600 = 81, and the square root of 81 is 9. Watch for adding the squares instead of subtracting. Or: recognise the 9, 40, 41 triple. Partial: adding instead of subtracting, giving √3,281, does not count. (8.G.B.7, finding a leg)","10 units. From A(−3, 2) to B(5, 8) the horizontal leg is 5 − (−3) = 8 and the vertical leg is 8 − 2 = 6, so 8<sup>2</sup> + 6<sup>2</sup> = 64 + 36 = 100 and the distance is 10. Or: recognise the 3, 4, 5 triple scaled by 2, or use the distance formula, which is the same theorem written out. Partial: the two legs found with no distance is approaching. (8.G.B.7, distance between two points on the coordinate plane)","17 in. For a box, length<sup>2</sup> + width<sup>2</sup> + height<sup>2</sup> = diagonal<sup>2</sup>, so 12<sup>2</sup> + 9<sup>2</sup> + 8<sup>2</sup> = 144 + 81 + 64 = 289, and the square root of 289 is 17. Or: find the floor diagonal first, √225 = 15, then 15² + 8² = 289. Partial: the floor diagonal only is approaching. (8.G.B.7, three dimensions)","Exactly the square root of 130, which is about 11.4 cm. 7<sup>2</sup> + 9<sup>2</sup> = 49 + 81 = 130, and the square root of 130 is 11.401…, which rounds to 11.4. Partial: one of the exact and the rounded answers given is approaching. (8.G.B.7, an answer that is not a whole number)","24 ft. The ladder is the hypotenuse, so 25<sup>2</sup> − 7<sup>2</sup> = 625 − 49 = 576, and the square root of 576 is 24. Or: recognise the 7, 24, 25 triple. Partial: adding instead of subtracting, giving √674, does not count. (8.G.B.7, finding a leg in a real-world problem)"]}]},{id:9,title:"Weigh the Memo",short:"The Memo",parts:[{std:["RI.8.8"],dur:140,dir:"Read the memo to yourself. Then answer out loud.",desc:"delineating an argument and judging whether the reasoning is sound and the evidence relevant and sufficient",html:memoText,qs:["What is Councilor Vasquez’s claim? What reasons does he give for it?","Which reason rests on the strongest evidence? Explain why that evidence is both relevant and sufficient.","One sentence in the memo is irrelevant evidence. Which one, and why does it not belong in this argument?","Two parts of the argument rest on weak reasoning. Name one of them and tell me what is wrong with the thinking."],key:["Claim: the $75,000 left in the lighting fund should shield the Cobb Road warehouse lot rather than replace the sixty river road lamps. Reasons: the lot is by far the brightest source on that side of town, measured at 18.3 against a town average of 19.9 and the river road’s 19.6; the owner’s matching offer means $42,000 buys 88 fixtures while the river road job is $71,000 for sixty; and the neighbors want it. Needs the claim and at least two reasons. (RI.8.8, delineating the argument)","Either the output reason or the cost reason, with the explanation. The output reason is relevant because it measures the very thing the money is meant to change, and sufficient because it compares all three places on one scale, 18.3 against 19.9 and 19.6, and explains what a 1.6 difference means. The cost reason is relevant because it prices both options and sufficient because it gives both totals and both counts, so a reader can work out $42,000 for 88 fixtures against $71,000 for sixty. (RI.8.8, relevant and sufficient evidence)","The sentence that 71 percent of residents describe the lantern walk as their favorite town event. How popular the lantern walk is has nothing to do with which lighting job the $75,000 should buy, so it supports neither the claim nor any of the reasons. (RI.8.8, recognizing irrelevant evidence)","Either one. The Brayford point: a vacancy rate that fell after the shielding does not show that the shielding caused it, because two years leaves room for many other causes and the memo rules out none of them. Or the opinion reason: the eleven people asked were the ones who came to a Cobb Road neighborhood meeting, so they are the residents most bothered by the lot and not a fair sample of the town, and being bothered is not evidence that this is the better use of the money. Needs the part named and what is wrong with the reasoning. (RI.8.8, soundness of reasoning)"]}]},{id:10,title:"Where They Disagree",short:"Conflict",parts:[{std:["RI.8.9"],dur:150,dir:"Read both texts to yourself. They are about the same warehouse lot. Then answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing two texts that conflict and identifying where they disagree on fact and where on interpretation",html:twoTexts,qs:["Name one point where the two texts disagree about a matter of fact. What exactly does each one say?","Name a second factual disagreement.","Name a point where they disagree about interpretation rather than fact. Explain the disagreement.","What evidence would settle one of the factual disagreements? Why could no evidence settle the interpretive one?"],key:["Either of these. The February sky reading taken 200 meters north of the lot: Boateng’s contractor got 18.9 on February 9, and the monitoring project’s fixed post got 18.3. Or the fixture count: Boateng says the lot carries 64 unshielded floodlights and that 24 canopy fixtures are already capped, while the monitoring project says it walked the parcel and found 88 fixtures with only nine of the twenty-four canopy caps intact. (RI.8.9, disagreement on a matter of fact)","The other one from the pair above. Boateng says 64 unshielded, with 24 canopy fixtures already capped. The monitoring project says 88 fixtures in all and fifteen of the canopy caps removed or broken, so those were running unshielded on the night of the reading. (RI.8.9, a second factual disagreement)","Both texts agree the highway interchange puts more light into the sky than the lot does. Boateng reads that as a reason the lot is the wrong target and the town should spend on the river road, where it owns every lamp. The monitoring project reads the same fact as beside the point, because the interchange is on the state’s budget and no town vote can change it, so the question is the largest source the council can act on. The fact is shared. What it means for the decision is not. (RI.8.9, disagreement on interpretation)","The reading could be settled by taking readings at the same post with both meters on the same nights, with the cloud and which bays were running written down each time, since the monitoring project itself says thin high cloud or idle east bays can move the number. The fixture count could be settled by walking the parcel together and counting the caps. No evidence settles the interpretive disagreement, because whether “largest source” or “largest source the town can act on” is the right test is a judgment about what the council is responsible for, and no reading or count decides that. (RI.8.9, what the disagreement rests on)"]}]},{id:11,title:"Clause Work",short:"Grammar",parts:[{std:["L.8.1"],dur:140,dir:"A verbal is a verb form doing another job. Answer out loud. When you fix a sentence, say the whole sentence the correct way.",desc:"verbals, active and passive voice, mood, and fixing a shift in voice",qs:["“Hanging the lanterns took the crew four hours.” Which word is the verbal? What kind is it, and what job does it do in the sentence?","“The councilor, reading from the memo, did not look up.” Find the verbal and name its kind. Who or what does it describe?","“Her plan was to pull the handle at nine.” Find the verbal and name its kind.","Say this sentence in active voice: “The old cabinet was opened by two linemen.”","Fix the verb: “I wish the sky over the park was darker.”","Fix the shift in voice: “Nadia pulled the handle, and then the new lamps were switched on by the crew.”","Name the mood of this sentence: “Check the reading before you leave.”"],key:["Hanging. Gerund. With its phrase, “Hanging the lanterns,” it is the subject of the sentence, so it acts as a noun. (L.8.1a, verbals)","reading. Participle. It describes the councilor, so it acts as an adjective. (L.8.1a, verbals)","to pull. Infinitive. (L.8.1a, verbals)","Two linemen opened the old cabinet. (L.8.1b, active and passive voice)","I wish the sky over the park were darker. A wish about something that is not true takes were. (L.8.1c, subjunctive mood)","Nadia pulled the handle, and then the crew switched on the new lamps. Both verbs are now active. (L.8.1d, shift in voice)","Imperative. It gives a command. (L.8.1c, mood)"]}]},{id:12,title:"Field Desk",short:"Write",parts:[{alt:{group:"write",key:"argument",label:"Argue",icon:"⚖️",hint:"River road or the warehouse lot?"},std:["W.8.1"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:240,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"an argument writing piece built from a fact box",html:()=>`<p class="big">The council has $75,000 left in the lighting fund and can pay for only one of these two jobs. Write an argument for the one it should choose. Use the fact box. Write 8 to 10 sentences.</p>${factBox("Fact box: two lighting jobs",["<strong>The river road.</strong> 60 unshielded lamps, $71,000, all of them owned by the town. About 240 people walk the road after dark on a usual night, and it is the only lit route between the school and the north neighborhood. The sky reading on that stretch is 19.6.","<strong>The Cobb Road warehouse lot.</strong> 88 fixtures, of which 64 or more are unshielded. The owner will pay half of the $84,000 job, so the town pays $42,000. About six people cross the lot after dark. The sky reading 200 meters north of the lot is 18.3 against a town average of 19.9.","On the magnitude scale a larger number means a darker sky, and a difference of 1.6 is a sky roughly four times brighter.","If the town does nothing, the river road has no scheduled date, and the warehouse says it will shield its own lot by 2029.","The money cannot be split. A crew has to be hired for a whole job."])}${checklist(["Introduce your claim and name the other side, and show how your claim is different","Back your claim with logical reasoning and relevant evidence from the fact box","Use words, phrases, and clauses that show how your reasons connect: because, however, as a result","Keep a formal style, with no slang","End with a conclusion that follows from and supports your argument"])}`,key:["Introduces the claim clearly and acknowledges the opposing claim, showing how the two differ (W.8.1a)","Organizes the reasons and evidence in an order that makes sense (W.8.1a)","Supports the claim with logical reasoning and relevant evidence from the fact box, such as 240 walkers against six, or $42,000 for 88 fixtures against $71,000 for sixty (W.8.1b)","Shows that the sources of the evidence are the fact box, and uses the numbers accurately rather than inventing them (W.8.1b)","Uses words, phrases, and clauses to connect the claim, the reasons, the evidence, and the opposing claim (W.8.1c)","Keeps a formal style and an objective tone, with no slang (W.8.1d)","Ends with a concluding statement that follows from and supports the argument (W.8.1e)"]},{alt:{group:"write",key:"narrative",label:"Story",icon:"📖",hint:"Nine o'clock on the roof, twelve blocks go dark"},std:["W.8.3"],n:7,label:"checks met",dur:240,dir:"Write on your paper for about 5 minutes. Then hold it up or read it to your teacher.",desc:"a narrative writing piece",html:()=>`<p class="big">You are on a roof at nine o’clock on the first clear night of January. At nine exactly, every street lamp for twelve blocks goes out, stays out for a few seconds, and comes back a different color. You knew it was going to happen. Nobody else up there did. Write the story of the next ten minutes. Write 10 to 12 sentences.</p>${checklist(["Set the scene: where you are, who is telling the story, and what you can see and hear","Tell the events in the order they happen","Include at least two lines of dialogue","Use details the reader can see, hear, or feel","Use time words that signal a shift: at first, a few seconds later, by the time, finally","End by showing what the narrator felt or understood by the end of the ten minutes"])}`,key:["Establishes a situation and a point of view and introduces the narrator and other characters (W.8.3a)","Events unfold in an order that is natural and logical (W.8.3a)","Uses narrative techniques such as dialogue, pacing, description, or reflection (W.8.3b)","Includes at least two lines of dialogue that move the story along (W.8.3b)","Uses transition words and phrases that show sequence and shifts in time (W.8.3c)","Uses precise words, descriptive details, and sensory language (W.8.3d)","Ends with a conclusion that follows from and reflects on the events (W.8.3e)"]}]},{id:13,title:"Source Desk",short:"Sources",parts:[{std:["W.8.8"],n:4,label:"checks met",dur:145,dir:"Read both sources. Answer numbers 1 and 2 out loud. Do numbers 3 and 4 on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"judging sources, choosing search terms, fixing plagiarism, and citing",html:()=>SOURCES()+`<ol class="qlist"><li>The two sources disagree about what a capped street lamp does. Which source would you trust? Give two reasons from the source information.</li><li>You want one more source. Which search terms are best, and why? A: streetlights. B: shielded street lighting glare pedestrian obstacle detection study. C: how to save money on electricity.</li><li>Spot the plagiarism. A student’s report says, with no quotation marks and no source named: <em>Glare, not total output, was the strongest single predictor of a missed obstacle.</em> Fix it on your paper. Quote it correctly, or paraphrase it and name the author.</li><li>Write a citation for Source A with the author, title, publisher, and year.</li></ol>`,key:["Source A. Two reasons, such as: it names an author who is a lighting engineer, it comes from a state research office, it carries a date and is recent, and it reports a measured result across 1,100 trials. Source B is an undated forum post by an unknown user who writes “I read somewhere” and “Pretty sure.” Full credit for two reasons drawn from the source lines. For the teacher: Source B is also wrong on the facts, because a cap redirects light downward rather than removing half of it. (W.8.8, credibility and accuracy)","B. It uses the key words of the research question, so the results will be about shielded lighting, glare, and what a walker can actually see. A is far too broad and C is off topic. (W.8.8, search terms)","Either fix earns the check. Quote: Dr. Hannah Ozturk reports that “glare, not total output, was the strongest single predictor of a missed obstacle.” Paraphrase: According to Dr. Hannah Ozturk, the thing that best predicted whether a walker missed an obstacle was glare rather than how much light the lamp produced. The student must name the source either way, and the quote must carry quotation marks. (W.8.8, quoting or paraphrasing without plagiarism)","Has all four parts. Example: Ozturk, Hannah. “Glare, Output, and Obstacle Detection: A Field Study of 1,100 Trials.” State Transportation Research Office, 2025. Any clear, consistent order is fine. (W.8.8, citation)"]}]},{id:14,title:"Brief the Council",short:"Brief",parts:[{std:["SL.8.4"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:135,dir:"Plan for 1 minute. Then talk for 1 to 2 minutes. Look at the camera and speak clearly.",desc:"a 1 to 2 minute spoken briefing that presents a claim and findings with evidence from a data table",html:()=>`<p class="big">The lighting fund has $75,000 left and the council has to spend it on one job. Your survey produced the table below. On the magnitude scale a larger number means a darker sky.</p>${bigTable(["","River road","Cobb Road lot"],[["Fixtures the job would shield","60","64"],["What the town pays","$71,000","$42,000, with the owner paying the rest"],["Cost to the town for each fixture","$1,183","$656"],["Sky reading at the nearest fixed post","19.6","18.3"],["People there after dark on a usual night","about 240","about 6"],["If the town does nothing","No date set","Owner shields it by 2029"],["Worth knowing","The only lit route from the school to the north neighborhood","The lot is lit all night and empty after 9 p.m."]],"Two jobs the lighting fund can pay for")}<p class="big" style="font-size:1.5rem">Brief the council. Say which job to fund, give your strongest evidence from the table, name the drawback of your choice, and end with what you need the council to do.</p><div class="row center" style="margin-top:16px;gap:14px">${["My recommendation","The evidence","The drawback","What I need from the council"].map(x=>`<span class="pill" style="font-size:1.1rem">${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`,key:["States a clear recommendation up front and stays focused on it. Either job can be recommended. The Cobb Road case rests on the sky reading of 18.3 against 19.6 and on $656 a fixture against $1,183. The river road case rests on about 240 people after dark against six and on the town controlling the schedule. (SL.8.4)","Emphasizes the most important points in a focused, coherent order rather than reading the whole table out (SL.8.4)","Backs the recommendation with relevant evidence and sound reasoning from the table, with the numbers used accurately (SL.8.4)","Uses well-chosen details, including the drawback. For Cobb Road, the lot is empty after 9 p.m. and the owner will shield it by 2029 anyway, so the town’s $42,000 mostly buys an earlier date. For the river road, the town pays nearly twice as much per fixture for a smaller change in the sky reading. (SL.8.4)","Appropriate eye contact with the camera, adequate volume, and clear pronunciation for 1 to 2 minutes (SL.8.4)"]}]},{id:15,title:"Ends or Repeats",short:"Rational?",parts:[{std:["8.NS.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"telling rational numbers from irrational ones, and turning a repeating decimal into a fraction",html:()=>lead("The monitoring post reads the sky as a decimal number. Not every decimal behaves the same way."),qs:["The post read 19.9. Is 19.9 rational or irrational? Write it as a fraction of two integers.","A meter that is drifting reads 0.272727..., repeating forever. Write that as a fraction.","Is the square root of 50 rational or irrational? Between which two whole numbers does it sit?"],key:["Rational. Its decimal ends, so it can be written as a fraction: 199/10. Or: 19 9/10 as a mixed number counts, as long as it is turned into a single fraction. (8.NS.A.1)","3/11. Let x = 0.272727... Then 100x = 27.272727... Subtracting gives 99x = 27, so x = 27/99 = 3/11. Check: 3 ÷ 11 = 0.272727... Or: name it from the pattern, since a two-digit repeat sits over 99. Partial: 27/99 not reduced still counts; the method is what is rated. (8.NS.A.1, converting a repeating decimal to a fraction)","Irrational, because 50 is not a perfect square, so its root is a decimal that never ends and never repeats. It sits between 7 and 8, because 7 squared is 49 and 8 squared is 64. Partial: irrational with no two whole numbers given, or the reverse, is approaching. (8.NS.A.1)"]}]},{id:16,title:"Powers and Roots",short:"Powers",parts:[{std:["8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2"],n:4,dur:60,dir:"Work on your paper and hold it up. Say each answer out loud.",desc:"applying the properties of integer exponents, and using square root and cube root symbols to solve equations",html:()=>lead("The scale the meter uses is built on powers, so the crew works with them often."),qs:["Write 3 to the second power times 3 to the negative fifth power as a single power of 3, then as a fraction.","Simplify 2 to the fourth power, all raised to the second power, divided by 2 to the fifth power.","Solve x squared = 196, and solve x cubed = 64.","Is the square root of 2 rational or irrational? How do you know?"],key:["3 to the negative third power, which is 1/27. Add the exponents when the bases match: 2 + (-5) = -3, and a negative exponent means the reciprocal. Or: 9 ÷ 243 = 1/27 worked out as numbers. Partial: the single power right with no fraction is approaching. (8.EE.A.1)","8. The power of a power multiplies the exponents, so it is 2 to the eighth. Dividing subtracts: 8 - 5 = 3, and 2 cubed is 8. Or: 16 squared is 256, and 256 ÷ 32 = 8 worked out as numbers. (8.EE.A.1)","x = 14 or x = -14, because both 14 squared and -14 squared give 196. A square root equation has two solutions when p is positive. x cubed = 64 has the one solution x = 4, because only 4 cubed is 64. Partial: x = 14 only leaves the negative root out and is approaching. (8.EE.A.2)","Irrational. 2 is not a perfect square, and the square root of a number that is not a perfect square cannot be written as a fraction of two integers. Its decimal never ends and never repeats. (8.EE.A.2)"]}]},{id:17,title:"Two Crews, Two Rates",short:"Slope",parts:[{std:["8.EE.B.5"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the graph. Work on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"reading slope as a unit rate and comparing two proportional relationships shown in different ways",html:()=>lead("Crew A's work is shown by the graph. Crew B replaces lamps at the rate given by the equation l = 20m, where l is lamps and m is months."),fig:()=>F.row(rateGraph()),qs:["What is the slope of Crew A's line, and what does it mean for the crew?","Which crew replaces lamps faster? How do you know?","If both crews work for 7 months, how many more lamps has the faster crew replaced?"],key:["15. Rise over run is 30 ÷ 2, or 120 ÷ 8, which is 15 either way. The slope is the unit rate: Crew A replaces 15 lamps a month. Partial: 15 given with no account of what it means for the crew is approaching. (8.EE.B.5, slope as a unit rate)","Crew B. Its rate is the 20 in l = 20m, which is 20 lamps a month, and 20 is more than 15. This compares a graph with an equation. Or: graph Crew B on the same axes and compare the steepness. Partial: the right crew with no two rates compared is approaching. (8.EE.B.5, comparing two proportional relationships in different forms)","35 more. Crew B does 20 × 7 = 140 and Crew A does 15 × 7 = 105, and 140 - 105 = 35. Or: the gap grows 5 lamps a month, so 7 months gives 35. Partial: both totals right with no difference taken is approaching. (8.EE.B.5)"]}]},{id:18,title:"Move the Panel",short:"Transform",parts:[{std:["8.G.A.3"],n:4,dur:65,dir:"Look at the triangle on the grid. Work on your paper and hold it up.",desc:"describing the effect of a translation, a reflection, a rotation, and a dilation on a figure using coordinates",html:()=>lead("A triangular lantern panel is drawn on the crew's grid with corners at (1, 1), (4, 1) and (1, 3)."),fig:()=>F.row([panelGrid(),"The panel on the grid"]),qs:["Translate the panel 3 units left and 2 units up. Give the coordinates of the three new corners.","Reflect the original panel across the horizontal axis. Give the coordinates of the three new corners.","Rotate the original panel 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin. Give the rule for what happens to a point, and give the coordinates of the three new corners.","Dilate the original panel from the origin by a scale factor of 2. Give the new coordinates, and say what happens to the side lengths and the angles."],key:["(-2, 3), (1, 3) and (-2, 5). A translation subtracts 3 from every first coordinate and adds 2 to every second one. Or: count the squares on the grid instead of using the rule. Partial: two of the three corners right is approaching. (8.G.A.3, translation)","(1, -1), (4, -1) and (1, -3). A reflection across the horizontal axis keeps the first coordinate and reverses the sign of the second. Partial: the three corners right with nothing said about the coordinates is approaching. (8.G.A.3, reflection)","The rule is (x, y) goes to (-y, x). (1, 1) goes to (-1, 1), (4, 1) goes to (-1, 4) and (1, 3) goes to (-3, 1). Check one: (4, 1) has y = 1, so the image starts with -1, and x = 4, so it ends with 4. A rotation keeps every side length and every angle, so the panel is congruent to the original. Or: trace the panel and turn the paper a quarter turn to the left, or call it a 270 degree clockwise turn. Partial: the rule right with a corner wrong, or three right corners with no rule, is approaching. (8.G.A.3, rotation)","(2, 2), (8, 2) and (2, 6). A dilation from the origin multiplies both coordinates by the scale factor. Every side length doubles and every angle stays the same, so the panel keeps its shape and changes only its size. Partial: the corners and the side lengths right with nothing said about the angles is approaching. (8.G.A.3, dilation)"]}]},{id:19,title:"Scatter the Readings",short:"Scatter",parts:[{std:["8.SP.A.1"],n:3,dur:45,dir:"Look at the scatter plot. Answer out loud.",desc:"interpreting a scatter plot for association, clustering, outliers, and the shape of the pattern",html:()=>lead("Volunteers counted the stars they could see by eye from eight places, and recorded how far each place was from the middle of town."),fig:()=>F.row(scatterFig()),qs:["Describe the association between the two quantities.","Is there an outlier? Give its coordinates and suggest why a point could sit there.","Would a straight line fit the rest of the points well? What does the pattern say about the town?"],key:["A positive association: as the distance from the middle of town goes up, the number of stars counted goes up too. Seven of the eight points rise steadily. Partial: positive named with no account of what goes up with what is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, positive association)","Yes. The point at about (2.2, 120) sits far above the others, which at that distance count around 45. Any sensible reason counts, such as the reading being taken on a night when a nearby lot's lights were off, a miscount, or a spot screened from the lamps by trees or a building. Partial: the point named with no possible reason is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, outliers)","Yes. Leaving the outlier out, the other seven points lie close to a straight rising line, so the association is roughly linear rather than curved. It says the town's own lamps are what is washing out the stars: step away from them and the count climbs steadily. Partial: a straight-line fit judged with nothing said about the town is approaching. (8.SP.A.1, linear association)"]}]},{id:20,title:"Explain the Scale",short:"Explain",parts:[{std:["W.8.2"],n:5,label:"checks met",dur:55,dir:"Write on your paper. Then hold it up and read it to your teacher.",desc:"an informative piece that explains a measurement scale and what one town's numbers mean",html:()=>`<p class="big">Write to explain to a reader who knows nothing about it how a sky quality meter reading works, and what Hollowell's move from 19.1 to 19.9 actually means. Use facts from the article. Write 6 to 8 sentences.</p>${factBox("Facts you can use",["A sky quality meter is pointed straight up and reads the brightness of one patch of sky in magnitudes per square arcsecond.","On that scale a larger number means a darker sky.","A reading of 18 is a bright suburban sky. A reading of 21.5 is a rural sky with the Milky Way plainly visible.","The scale is not linear: one magnitude is a change of about two and a half times in brightness.","Hollowell's fixed post rose from 19.1 to 19.9 after 420 lamps were replaced, which is a sky about twice as dark.","19.9 is still well short of 21.5, and the town is ringed by lights it does not own."])}${checklist(["Introduce the topic and preview what you will cover","Develop it with facts and definitions from the box","Use transitions to link one idea to the next","Use precise words: magnitude, scale, reading, linear","Keep a formal style and end with a concluding statement"])}`,key:["Introduces the topic clearly and previews what follows (W.8.2)","Develops the topic with relevant facts and definitions from the box, in the student's own sentences. The key fact to get right is that a larger number means a darker sky (W.8.2)","Uses transitions to create cohesion and clarify the relationships between the ideas (W.8.2)","Uses precise language and domain-specific vocabulary, such as magnitude, scale, reading, linear (W.8.2)","Keeps a formal style and provides a concluding statement that follows from the information (W.8.2)","Worth noting on the record sheet: an answer that treats the 0.8 rise as a small change has missed the point of the non-linear scale, and one that calls 19.9 a rural sky has missed the last fact in the box."]}]},{id:21,title:"Where the Ideas Came From",short:"Ideas",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.1"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing the ideas behind American constitutional democracy to their sources",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three writers whose ideas the founders argued from.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Writer</th><th>The idea</th></tr>
     <tr><td>John Locke</td><td>people hold natural rights to life, liberty and property, and government exists by their agreement to protect them</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Baron de Montesquieu</td><td>liberty survives only where the power to make, to carry out and to judge the law sit in separate hands</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Jean-Jacques Rousseau</td><td>legitimate authority rests on a compact among the people themselves rather than on inheritance or conquest</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("lantern",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}</div>`,qs:["Match each writer to a specific sentence or structure in the Declaration of Independence or the Constitution.","Locke said people may replace a government that fails to protect their rights. Explain how that sentence changes what a revolution is.","None of these three was American and none lived to see the Constitution. Why would a new country build on borrowed ideas rather than its own?"],key:["Locke to the Declaration's unalienable rights and to governments deriving their just powers from the consent of the governed. Montesquieu to the three articles of the Constitution establishing a separate legislature, executive and judiciary with checks on each other. Rousseau to the idea of a people constituting a government for themselves, visible in the Preamble's we the people. Partial: two of the three writers matched to a document is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","It turns revolution from a crime into a remedy. If government is a trust held on terms, then breaking the terms ends the obligation to obey, and the people are not rebelling against lawful authority but withdrawing authority that has already failed. That is exactly the structure of the Declaration's argument. Partial: naming consent with nothing said about withdrawing authority rather than rebelling is approaching. (HSS-8.1)","Because the colonists were not trying to invent a new philosophy, they were trying to win an argument. Appealing to widely respected writers made their case sound like established principle rather than novelty, and it addressed an educated transatlantic audience in terms that audience already accepted. Their own contribution was putting the ideas into working machinery. Partial: saying they copied the writers with nothing said about winning an argument is approaching. (HSS-8.1)"]},{std:["HSS-8.2"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the amendment process and what the difficulty of amending protects",html:()=>`<p class="big">How the Constitution is changed. Every amendment so far has taken the first path.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Step</th><th>Path one</th><th>Path two</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Proposed by</td><td>two thirds of each house of Congress</td><td>a convention called by two thirds of the states</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Ratified by</td><td>three quarters of the state legislatures</td><td>conventions in three quarters of the states</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">About 11,000 amendments have been proposed in Congress. Twenty seven have been adopted, and ten of those were the Bill of Rights, ratified in one batch in 1791.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("key",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["Both steps need a supermajority, and the second one is of the states rather than of voters. What is that design protecting against?","Seventeen amendments in more than two centuries, outside the Bill of Rights. Is that a strength or a weakness? Argue one side.","If the Constitution changes this rarely, how does the document keep up with a country that changes constantly?"],key:["Against a temporary majority rewriting the basic rules, and against the largest states amending the Constitution over the objection of the rest. Requiring agreement across most of the country and across time means a change has to be broadly and durably wanted, not merely popular in one place in one season. Partial: one of the two guards named is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","Any argued side. Strength: the basic structure is stable, rights cannot be stripped by one angry session, and people can rely on the rules. Weakness: plainly needed changes can be blocked by a small minority of states, and the country has to fall back on court interpretation, which is less democratic than amending. The argument is what is rated. Partial: a side chosen with no argument is approaching. (HSS-8.2)","Mostly through interpretation and implied powers: courts read the existing words against new situations, and Congress reaches new subjects through the commerce clause and the necessary and proper clause. That keeps the document usable and it also means a great deal of constitutional change is made by judges and legislators rather than by amendment. Partial: naming amendment as the only route is approaching, since most change has come another way. (HSS-8.2)"]},{std:["HSS-8.3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing the expansion of the right to vote and who remained excluded at each step",html:()=>`<p class="big">Who could vote, and when that changed.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period or amendment</th><th>Change</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1790</td><td>mostly white men who met a property requirement</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1820s and 1830s</td><td>states drop property requirements for white men</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Fifteenth Amendment, 1870</td><td>the vote may not be denied on account of race</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Nineteenth Amendment, 1920</td><td>the vote may not be denied on account of sex</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Twenty Fourth Amendment, 1964</td><td>poll taxes abolished in federal elections</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Twenty Sixth Amendment, 1971</td><td>voting age lowered to eighteen</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("hands",74)}${ico("star",74)}${ico("flag",74)}</div>`,qs:["Several states actually took the vote away from free Black men during the 1820s and 1830s while extending it to white men without property. What does that show about how the expansion worked?","The Fifteenth Amendment was ratified in 1870, yet a Twenty Fourth Amendment about poll taxes was still needed in 1964. Explain the gap.","Each step on this chart was won by people who did not yet have the vote. How do you organize for a right you cannot vote yourself?"],key:["That it was not a single widening of the franchise but a series of separate political fights, each with its own winners and losers. The same conventions that removed property tests for white men wrote race into the qualifications, so one group gained exactly when another lost. Progress in a system like this is not automatic or in one direction. Partial: noting the widening with nothing said about who lost the vote at the same time is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","Because the amendment barred denying the vote on account of race, and states found requirements that never mentioned race: poll taxes, literacy tests, grandfather clauses and residency rules, applied unevenly by local officials. A right stated in general terms can be hollowed out by procedure, which is why later amendments and statutes had to name the specific devices. Partial: naming the devices with nothing said about a general right being hollowed out by procedure is approaching. (HSS-8.3)","By using the rights you do have: petition, assembly, the press, the courts, strikes and boycotts, and persuading people who can vote. Suffrage campaigns ran newspapers, marched, lobbied legislators, filed test cases and won state by state before any amendment passed. A strong answer names at least two of those and explains why they create pressure. Partial: one route named is approaching, and saying nothing could be done is approaching too. (HSS-8.3)"]}]},{id:22,title:"An Informed Public",short:"New Nation",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.4"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the aspiration to an informed public and the institutions built to serve it",html:()=>`<p class="big">Americans built an unusual amount of machinery for spreading information.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Institution</th><th>What it was</th></tr>
     <tr><td>cheap postage for newspapers</td><td>Congress carried newspapers at a fraction of the cost of a letter, so papers circulated far from where they were printed</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the penny press, from the 1830s</td><td>newspapers priced at one cent instead of six, sold on the street rather than by subscription</td></tr>
     <tr><td>lyceums</td><td>local societies that hired lecturers on science, history and reform; some three thousand towns had one by 1835</td></tr>
     <tr><td>subscription and then free public libraries</td><td>pooled book collections, first paid for by members and later by towns</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("radio",74)}${ico("lamp",74)}</div>`,qs:["Why would Congress subsidize the mailing of newspapers but not of letters?","A penny paper sold on the street reaches a different reader than a six cent subscription. Name two consequences of that change.","Name one limit on how informed this public actually was."],key:["Because a newspaper informs many people about public affairs while a letter is private business, and the stated reason was that a republic depends on voters knowing what their government is doing. It is an early case of public money spent to create an informed electorate, and it also meant the government had some influence over what circulated. Partial: noting the cheap rate with nothing said about an informed electorate is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","Any two: far more people read a paper, including wage workers who could not afford a subscription; papers paid for themselves by advertising and street sales rather than by party subsidy; content shifted toward crime, scandal and human interest to hold a mass audience; and politicians had to address a much wider public than before. Partial: one change named is approaching. (HSS-8.4)","Any accurate limit: literacy was far from universal and was illegal to teach to enslaved people in most Southern states; papers were openly partisan and often printed rumor as fact; lyceums and libraries were concentrated in the Northeast and in towns rather than on the frontier; and most of this machinery served white readers. Partial: one limit named is approaching. (HSS-8.4)"]},{std:["HSS-8.5"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the causes and results of the War of 1812",html:()=>`<p class="big">The War of 1812, in two columns.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Why it came</th><th>What it settled</th></tr>
     <tr><td>British ships stopped American vessels and seized sailors</td><td>the Treaty of Ghent, 1814, returned the borders to exactly where they had been</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Britain seized American cargoes bound for France</td><td>impressment stopped in practice, because Britain's war with France had ended</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Americans believed Britain was arming Native nations in the Northwest</td><td>Native resistance in the Northwest was broken, opening that land to settlement</td></tr>
     <tr><td>some members of Congress wanted Canada</td><td>Canada stayed British; invasions failed</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("anchor",74)}${ico("flag",74)}${ico("map",74)}</div>`,qs:["The treaty changed no borders and settled none of the stated grievances in writing. Why did Americans still treat the war as a success?","Who lost the most in this war, and why is that group easy to leave out of the account?","New England merchants opposed this war strongly, even talking of leaving the Union. Why would the section most hurt by British seizures oppose fighting Britain?"],key:["Because the country had survived a war with the strongest navy in the world, held its territory, and won late victories that felt decisive. Impressment ended anyway once Britain's European war finished, so the grievance disappeared without needing a clause. National confidence rose sharply and the opposition party collapsed, which made it feel like a win. Partial: noting the confidence with nothing said about impressment ending anyway is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","The Native nations of the Northwest and the Southeast, whose confederation was broken and whose land was opened to American settlement in the years that followed. They are easy to leave out because the treaty was between two governments neither of which represented them, so their loss appears nowhere in the written settlement even though it was the war's most lasting result. Partial: naming the nations with nothing said about why they appear nowhere in the treaty is approaching. (HSS-8.5)","Because war with their main trading partner stopped their trade altogether, and seizures cost them some ships while a war and a blockade cost them all of them. They also had no interest in acquiring Canada or western land, which were the war aims of the South and the West, so they saw themselves asked to pay for someone else's expansion. Partial: naming the lost trade with nothing said about the war aims they had no interest in is approaching. (HSS-8.5)"]},{std:["HSS-8.6"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing early labor organizing by mill workers in the Northeast",html:()=>`<p class="big">When the Lowell mills cut wages in 1834, about eight hundred women walked out together, and when the company raised the rent on the boarding houses in 1836, some fifteen hundred did. Neither turnout won. In 1844 the workers formed the Lowell Female Labor Reform Association, elected Sarah Bagley as president, and shifted tactics: they gathered thousands of signatures for a ten hour day and testified before a Massachusetts legislative committee, the first time in the state's history that workers gave evidence about their own conditions. The committee declined to act. New Hampshire passed a ten hour law in 1847 and Massachusetts not until much later.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("clock",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["Walking out failed twice and the association switched to petitions and testimony. Why would that be a better tool for these workers?","The mill women were young, unmarried and living together in company boarding houses. How would that make organizing easier, and how would it make it riskier?","They lost in Massachusetts and a smaller state passed the law first. Why might that be?"],key:["Because the company could replace walkouts from a steady supply of new hires from the farms, so withholding labor gave little leverage. A petition campaign and public testimony moved the fight to a body the company did not control, put the conditions on the public record, and gathered allies beyond the mill. It was a change from economic pressure to political pressure. Partial: naming the petition with nothing said about why withholding labor gave little leverage is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Easier because they lived and ate together, so word spread in one evening and nobody had a separate household to protect. Riskier because the company owned the housing as well as the job, so being fired meant losing your room and your place in town on the same day, and a blacklist followed you to the next mill. Partial: one of the easier and the riskier given is approaching. (HSS-8.6)","Because Massachusetts mill owners had great influence in their own legislature, and the state's economy depended on them, so the political cost of crossing them was high. A smaller state with fewer large mills had less at stake and fewer powerful objectors. Where an industry is concentrated, it is strongest politically. Partial: noting the difference with nothing said about where an industry is concentrated is approaching. (HSS-8.6)"]}]},{id:23,title:"Divided Ground",short:"Sections",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.7"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the range of work enslaved people were forced to perform in the South",html:()=>`<p class="big">Cotton was the largest use of enslaved labor and far from the only one.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Work</th><th>Where</th><th>Conditions</th></tr>
     <tr><td>rice</td><td>coastal South Carolina and Georgia</td><td>flooded fields, worked by a task system; workers who finished a set task had the rest of the day</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sugar</td><td>southern Louisiana</td><td>a short, punishing harvest with mills running day and night</td></tr>
     <tr><td>skilled trades</td><td>towns and plantations</td><td>blacksmiths, carpenters, coopers, seamstresses; often hired out, with the wage going to the owner</td></tr>
     <tr><td>household work</td><td>everywhere</td><td>cooking, nursing, sewing, on call at all hours</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("pan",74)}${ico("box",74)}</div>`,qs:["Under the task system a worker who finished early had the rest of the day. What could a family do with that time, and why would an owner allow it?","A hired out blacksmith earned real wages that went to his owner. What was unusual about his position, and what did it not change?","Name two skills that were common among enslaved people and that the South's economy depended on."],key:["Families used it to grow and sell food, fish, hunt, make goods and sometimes accumulate small amounts of money, occasionally enough to buy a relative's freedom. Owners allowed it because the task system got the work done with less supervision, and because people with something of their own were less likely to run. It was a concession extracted by people who had no rights at all. Partial: one of the families' use and the owners' reason given is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","He moved about town, negotiated with employers, handled money and often lived apart, so his daily life looked more like a free worker's than a field hand's, and some such men bought their own freedom over years. What it did not change was his legal status: he was still property, could be sold at any time, and had no claim on his own earnings. Partial: one of what it changed and what it did not is approaching. (HSS-8.7)","Any two with a plausible account: blacksmithing, carpentry, coopering, bricklaying, boat piloting, nursing and midwifery, spinning and sewing, and the whole body of knowledge about rice cultivation that West African workers brought and Carolina planters relied on. The region's claim that enslaved people were unskilled is contradicted by what it had them do. Partial: one skill named is approaching. (HSS-8.7)"]},{std:["HSS-8.8"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Read the note. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing manifest destiny as an idea and what it was used to justify",html:()=>`<p class="big">In 1845 a magazine editor named John O'Sullivan wrote that it was the United States' manifest destiny to spread across the continent. The phrase caught on immediately. It held three claims at once: that American expansion was inevitable, that it was good for the people already living there, and that it carried self government westward. The same year Texas was annexed; within three years the United States had settled the Oregon boundary with Britain and taken half of Mexico's territory.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("map",74)}${ico("sun",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["Take the three claims one at a time and say what each one does for someone arguing in favor of expansion.","Who had reason to reject the second claim, and on what evidence?","The phrase appeared in 1845, after decades of expansion. What does it tell you that the slogan came after the fact?"],key:["Inevitability removes the question of whether to expand and leaves only how, so opposition looks futile. Benefit to others turns taking land into a service, which answers the moral objection. Carrying self government westward ties expansion to the nation's founding ideals, which makes opposing it look unpatriotic. Together they convert a choice into a duty. Partial: two of the three parts of the phrase explained is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","Native nations, who lost their land, their treaties and in many cases their lives, and Mexican residents of the ceded territory, who were promised citizenship and property and largely lost the property. The evidence is removal, the collapse of the California Native population, and the failure of the land title guarantees in the 1848 treaty. Partial: one group named with its evidence is approaching. (HSS-8.8)","That the slogan was a justification rather than a plan. Expansion was already happening for ordinary reasons of land, harbors, trade and politics, and the phrase gave it a flattering story afterward. A strong answer notices that naming something a destiny is a way of not having to defend it. Partial: taking the slogan as the cause of expansion is the misreading to watch for and is approaching. (HSS-8.8)"]},{std:["HSS-8.9"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing the antislavery petition campaign and the gag rule in Congress",html:()=>`<p class="big">The Constitution guarantees a right to petition Congress. Abolitionists used it hard.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>What happened</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1835 to 1836</td><td>antislavery societies send Congress hundreds of thousands of signatures, many gathered door to door by women</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1836</td><td>the House adopts a rule tabling every antislavery petition without reading or debate</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1836 to 1844</td><td>John Quincy Adams, a former president now in the House, reads and submits them anyway, session after session</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1844</td><td>the House repeals the rule</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("pencil",74)}${ico("books",74)}${ico("hands",74)}</div>`,qs:["The rule was meant to make the slavery question go away in Congress. Explain why it did the opposite.","Much of the signature gathering was done by women, who could not vote. Why would petitioning suit them, and what did the experience lead to?","Adams opposed slavery but spent eight years arguing mainly about the right to petition rather than about slavery itself. Why choose that ground?"],key:["Because it turned a disputed question into a clear violation of a right every member claimed to respect, which brought in people who were not abolitionists but did object to Congress refusing to read its own mail. Each year of the fight kept slavery on the floor, and the rule gave the movement a grievance that was far easier to defend publicly. Partial: naming the rule with nothing said about who it brought over to the cause is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","Petitioning was a form of political action open to people without the vote, and gathering signatures door to door used networks women already had. The experience taught them organizing, public speaking and committee work, and many of the women who ran these drives went on to lead the women's rights movement, including the 1848 convention at Seneca Falls. Partial: naming the petitions with nothing said about what the organizing led to is approaching. (HSS-8.9)","Because on the petition question he could win votes that he could never have won on slavery, including from members who disliked abolitionists. Defending a procedural right let him keep the subject alive in the chamber for eight years, and when the rule fell the petitions came back with the movement far larger than before. Partial: naming his persistence with nothing said about the votes a procedural fight could win is approaching. (HSS-8.9)"]}]},{id:24,title:"What Each Side Wanted",short:"War and After",parts:[{std:["HSS-8.10"],n:3,dur:100,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"comparing the stated aims of the two governments with the actions of enslaved people",html:()=>`<p class="big">Three accounts of what the war was for, all from the war years.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Who</th><th>What they said or did</th></tr>
     <tr><td>The seceding states, 1860 to 1861</td><td>their declarations of causes named the threat to slavery as the reason for leaving</td></tr>
     <tr><td>The United States government, 1861</td><td>Congress resolved that the war was to preserve the Union, not to interfere with slavery</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Enslaved people, from 1861 onward</td><td>hundreds of thousands walked off plantations toward Union lines as armies came near, and about 180,000 men enlisted once they could</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("flag",74)}${ico("hands",74)}${ico("star",74)}</div>`,qs:["Rows one and two give different reasons for the same war. Explain how both can be accurate.","Row three is not a statement, it is an action. What were those people doing to the war aims in row two?","By 1863 the United States government's stated aim had changed. Using all three rows, explain why."],key:["Because the two sides were answering different questions. The seceding states were explaining why they left, and the answer was slavery. The United States was explaining why it would fight, and in 1861 it needed the loyal slave states and Northern voters who cared about the Union but not about abolition. The cause of the conflict and a government's war aim are not the same thing. Partial: treating the two statements as contradicting each other, rather than answering different questions, is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Overriding them. Every person who reached Union lines forced officers and then Congress to decide what to do, and returning them meant strengthening the enemy's labor force. Enslaved people made their own freedom the practical question of the war long before the government was willing to call it a war aim. Partial: describing the escapes with nothing said about the decisions they forced is approaching. (HSS-8.10)","Because the war was not being won on the 1861 terms and because the third row made the old aim unworkable. Freedom was already happening wherever armies went, emancipation struck at the Confederacy's labor supply, it removed any chance of European recognition, and it opened the army to a large new source of soldiers. The aim changed because the facts on the ground changed it. Partial: one reason for the change named is approaching. (HSS-8.10)"]},{std:["HSS-8.11"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing sharecropping and farm debt as the economic outcome of Reconstruction",html:()=>`<p class="big">Freedpeople wanted land. Almost none got any, so most ended up farming someone else's.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>How it worked</th></tr>
     <tr><td>sharecropping</td><td>a family worked a plot and gave the landowner a third to a half of the crop</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the furnish</td><td>seed, tools and food were advanced on credit by the landowner or a store, at high interest</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the crop lien</td><td>the lender held a legal claim on the coming crop, so it could not be sold to anyone else</td></tr>
     <tr><td>settling up</td><td>the lender kept the accounts and did the arithmetic; many families ended the year owing more than they had earned</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("wheat",74)}${ico("coin",74)}${ico("books",74)}</div>`,qs:["Explain, row by row, how a family could work all year and finish deeper in debt.","Sharecropping was not slavery. Name two real differences, and then name what it had in common.","Proposals to give freedpeople land were made and dropped. Why would Congress hesitate, and what difference would land have made?"],key:["The family gives away up to half the crop in rent, buys everything it needs on credit at high interest, cannot sell the crop to a better buyer because the lien ties it to one lender, and then has the accounts settled by that same lender with no way to check the figures. Each row removes one way out, and together they produce debt that carries into the next year. Partial: two or three of the rows explained is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Differences: the family was not owned, could not be sold, kept its own household together, chose its own work rhythms on the plot, and could in principle move at the end of a year. In common: no land, no capital, no bargaining power, and a legal and financial structure that kept the labor on the same land for the same families' benefit. Partial: one of the differences and the things in common given is approaching. (HSS-8.11)","Congress hesitated because taking land from owners, even owners who had rebelled, cut against property rights that many members held sacred, and because support for Reconstruction was already draining away. Land would have given freedpeople an independent living, something to borrow against, and a base for political power, which is precisely why it was the one thing they asked for most and got least. Partial: one of the hesitation and what land would have given is approaching. (HSS-8.11)"]},{std:["HSS-8.12"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing late century immigration, its economic role and the political reaction to it",html:()=>`<p class="big">Immigration and the laws that answered it.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Period or law</th><th></th></tr>
     <tr><td>1870s</td><td>about 2.8 million arrivals, mostly from Ireland, Germany, Britain and Scandinavia</td></tr>
     <tr><td>1880s to 1900</td><td>arrivals shift toward Italy, Poland, Russia, Austria-Hungary and Greece</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Chinese Exclusion Act, 1882</td><td>barred almost all immigration from China, the first American law excluding a nationality</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Ellis Island, 1892, and Angel Island, 1910</td><td>federal inspection stations, one in New York and one in San Francisco bay</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("sailboat",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("truck",74)}</div>`,qs:["Industry in these years needed enormous numbers of workers. Explain how immigration and industrial growth fed each other.","The 1882 law excluded one nationality by name. What arguments were made for it, and what does its passage tell you about the politics of the period?","Angel Island and Ellis Island were both inspection stations and they were not the same experience. Why would that be?"],key:["Factories, mines, mills and railroads needed labor faster than the native born population could supply it, and wages that looked low in America were far above what was available in much of Europe, so the demand pulled people in. Those workers then built the rail and steel capacity that created still more jobs, and the growing cities created demand for everything made. Partial: one direction of the link given is approaching, since the growth and the arrivals fed each other. (HSS-8.12)","Supporters argued that Chinese workers lowered wages and would not assimilate, and Western labor organizations pushed hardest for it. Its passage shows that economic anxiety and organized racism could combine into federal law, and that a group whose labor had built the western railroads had no political protection once it was no longer needed. It set the precedent for later national quotas. Partial: naming the arguments with nothing said about what the law shows is approaching. (HSS-8.12)","Because the law they enforced was different. Most European arrivals at Ellis Island were presumed admissible and cleared in hours, while Chinese arrivals at Angel Island had to prove an exemption from the exclusion law and were held for weeks or months while their claims were investigated. The same federal process produced two very different experiences because the statute told it to. Partial: noting the different treatment with nothing said about the statute behind it is approaching. (HSS-8.12)"]}]},{id:25,title:"Eyes at the Lamp",short:"Heredity",parts:[{std:["LS3"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Look at the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"tracing a change in a gene through the protein it codes for to the trait that results",html:()=>`<p class="big">The moths that gather at the town's lamps carry four versions of one gene. It codes for a protein that carries dark pigment into the cells of the eye.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Gene version</th><th>Protein the gene codes for</th><th>Eye</th></tr>
     <tr><td>M1, unchanged</td><td>full length carrier, moves pigment at the normal rate</td><td>dark red all over</td></tr>
     <tr><td>M2, one base changed in the opening the pigment passes through</td><td>full length carrier that moves pigment at a tenth the rate</td><td>pale pink all over</td></tr>
     <tr><td>M3, one base changed so a stop signal replaces an amino acid</td><td>only half the protein is built, and it carries nothing</td><td>white all over</td></tr>
     <tr><td>M4, change in the switch stretch, so the gene is read only at the edge of the eye</td><td>the same full length carrier, built in some cells and not others</td><td>dark red at the edge, white in the middle</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("butterfly",74)}${ico("lamp",74)}</div>`,qs:["Follow M3 from the changed base to a white eye, naming what happens at each stage.","M2 builds a full length carrier and the eye is still pale. Say what is wrong with the protein, and what that shows about where inside a gene a change falls.","M4 leaves the protein itself completely unchanged. Explain how a moth ends up with two eye colors at once."],key:["The changed base turns a triplet that named an amino acid into a stop signal, so the ribosome halts halfway and only half the chain is built. Half a carrier does not fold into a working channel, so no pigment is carried into the eye cells, and cells with no pigment look white. Changed base, early stop, half a protein, no transport, no pigment, white eye. Partial: a chain that jumps from the changed base to the white eye with the half built carrier left out is approaching. (LS3)","The change sits in the opening the pigment has to squeeze through, so the protein is the right length and reaches the right place but its channel is the wrong shape and passes pigment ten times more slowly. Position inside the gene is what matters: a change in the stretch that builds the working surface costs most of the function, while the same kind of change elsewhere in the chain may cost nothing. Partial: saying the carrier works more slowly, with nothing said about where inside the gene the change fell mattering, is approaching. (LS3)","The change is in the switch stretch, which decides where and when the gene is read rather than what protein it makes. The gene is now read in the cells at the edge of the eye and not in the middle, so edge cells build the carrier and fill with pigment while middle cells build none and stay white. Changing where a gene is switched on changes the trait even when the protein is identical. Partial: saying the gene is read in some cells and not others, with nothing said about the switch stretch deciding where it is read, is approaching. (LS3)"]}]},{id:26,title:"Wings and the Riverbank",short:"Evolution",parts:[{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"telling homology from similar function and reading the fossil sequence as evidence of ancestry",html:()=>`<p class="big">A cut in the riverbank at the edge of town. Layers pile up newest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Rock</th><th>Fossils in it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1, 0 to 2 m</td><td>river sand</td><td>mussels and willow leaves like those alive today</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2, 2 to 10 m</td><td>clay</td><td>a flightless bird with long arm bones and claws on three fingers</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3, 10 to 22 m</td><td>sandstone</td><td>a small dinosaur with feathers along its arms and a long bony tail</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4, 22 to 36 m</td><td>mudstone</td><td>a reptile with scaly skin, no feathers, and the same arm bone arrangement</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Three wings that all keep an animal in the air.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Wing</th><th>What the surface is</th><th>Bones inside it</th><th>Joints inside it</th></tr>
     <tr><td>bat</td><td>skin stretched between very long finger bones</td><td>1 upper arm, 2 forearm, 5 digits</td><td>shoulder, elbow, wrist</td></tr>
     <tr><td>bird</td><td>feathers on a shortened, partly fused hand</td><td>1 upper arm, 2 forearm, fused wrist and digits</td><td>shoulder, elbow, wrist</td></tr>
     <tr><td>dragonfly</td><td>a thin sheet of hardened outer covering with tubes running through it</td><td>none</td><td>none</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("bluebird",74)}${ico("butterfly",74)}${ico("mountain",74)}</div>`,qs:["Read the riverbank layers from oldest to youngest and describe what appears when, paying attention to the arm bones.","Two of the three wings point to a close relationship and one does not. Say which, and name exactly what in the chart decides it.","Bird and bat embryos both grow five separate digits before the wing takes shape, and a dragonfly embryo never grows any. Say what that adds to the wing chart."],key:["Oldest first: layer 4, a scaly reptile with no feathers but with one upper arm bone, two forearm bones and digits; layer 3, a small dinosaur with feathers on its arms and a long bony tail; layer 2, a flightless bird with long arm bones and clawed fingers; layer 1, the living forms. The arm bone arrangement is the same all the way down, while feathers appear partway up the column in an animal that could not fly, and the tail shortens later. Feathers came before flight, and the limb was inherited and then reworked. Partial: the four layers in the right order with nothing said about feathers appearing in an animal that could not fly is approaching. (LS4)","The bat and the bird. Both wings are built from one upper arm bone, two forearm bones, then wrist and digits, with shoulder, elbow and wrist joints in the same order, even though one spreads skin and the other spreads feathers. The dragonfly's wing has no bones and no joints inside it at all, so there is nothing in common to inherit. What decides it is the bone and joint columns, not the fact that all three fly: a shared job can be met by completely different structures, and here it was. Partial: naming the bat and the bird with nothing said about the dragonfly wing having no bones or joints to inherit is approaching. (LS4)","It shows the shared bone plan is laid down by shared inherited instructions, before either animal's wing has a shape at all, so it cannot be a response to flying. The dragonfly never follows those instructions because it never had them. So the resemblance between bat and bird runs back to development and therefore to ancestry, while the resemblance between either of them and the dragonfly is only in what the wing does. Partial: saying the embryos grow the same digits, with nothing said about that ruling out flight as the cause of the resemblance, is approaching. (LS4)"]},{std:["LS4"],n:3,dur:90,dir:"Study both sets of numbers. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"explaining natural selection from a trait distribution and comparing it with cloned stock",html:()=>`<p class="big">A fungus reached the town's elms. Each new planting is grown from seed collected off the trees still standing in town, and 240 young trees are scored at ten years old.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>How the tree fared</th><th>Planting 1</th><th>Planting 4</th><th>Planting 9</th></tr>
     <tr><td>died within two years</td><td>158</td><td>92</td><td>31</td></tr>
     <tr><td>survived, lost branches</td><td>61</td><td>103</td><td>97</td></tr>
     <tr><td>survived with no visible damage</td><td>21</td><td>45</td><td>112</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The nursery also took cuttings from the three healthiest trees and sold those, so every tree it sold was a genetic copy of one of the three. They all shrugged off the first fungus. When a new strain arrived, nine in ten of them died in a single season.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("tree",74)}${ico("sprout",74)}${ico("leaf",74)}</div>`,qs:["Seed for each planting comes from the trees still standing. Explain why that produces the change in the chart.","Work out the share surviving with no visible damage in planting 1 and in planting 9, then say what the chart shows about the population and what it says about any one tree.","The nursery's cuttings all resisted the first fungus and nine in ten died when a new strain came. Explain that, and name what the seed grown trees had that the cuttings did not."],key:["Resistance already varies among elms and is inherited. The fungus kills the least resistant young trees, so the trees left standing to set seed are the more resistant ones, and the next planting is grown from their seed. Each round therefore starts from a more resistant set of parents, and the share dying within two years falls from 158 to 92 to 31 out of 240. Variation, a difference in which trees survive to reproduce, then inheritance. Partial: two of the three steps given is approaching, and the inheritance step is usually the one left out. (LS4)","Planting 1: 21 of 240, which is about 9 percent. Planting 9: 112 of 240, which is about 47 percent. The chart shows the distribution of resistance across the population shifting. It says nothing about any single tree becoming more resistant during its life, because a tree's resistance is fixed by the genes it germinated with. Partial: working out the 9 and 47 percent with nothing said about no individual tree having become more resistant is approaching. (LS4)","Every cutting carried the same genes, so it carried the same resistances and the same gaps. The first fungus met a resistance all of them had; the new strain met a gap all of them had, and with no variation between them there was no tree that happened to resist it. The seed grown trees had variation: each is a different combination of gene versions, so a new threat is likely to meet at least a few trees that can withstand it. Variation is what lets a population survive a change nobody planned for. Partial: saying the cuttings were all identical, with nothing said about the seed grown trees having variation for a new threat to meet, is approaching. (LS4)"]}]},{id:27,title:"Timing the Sky",short:"Universe",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the record. Answer out loud.",desc:"explaining the daily and yearly cycles from timed observations and naming gravity's part",html:()=>`<p class="big">For four months the crew noted the clock time at which one chosen star crossed due south.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Date</th><th>Time the star crossed due south</th></tr>
     <tr><td>January 1</td><td>10:00 p.m.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>February 1</td><td>8:02 p.m.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>March 1</td><td>6:08 p.m.</td></tr>
     <tr><td>April 1</td><td>4:06 p.m.</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("star",74)}${ico("clock",74)}${ico("lantern",74)}</div>`,qs:["The star crosses due south about two hours earlier each month. Explain why, and say where the pattern would stand after a full year.","The sun comes back to due south at about the same clock time every day while the star does not. Explain the difference.","Name the force that keeps Earth on its yearly path, and say what would have to be different about the sun for the year to be shorter."],key:["Earth turns once in about 23 hours 56 minutes relative to the stars, while a clock day is 24 hours, so a star crosses due south about four minutes earlier each day, which is about two hours earlier each month. After twelve months the accumulated shift is 24 hours, a whole day, so the star is back to crossing due south at 10:00 p.m. That is why the same constellations return at the same time each year. Partial: naming the four minute daily shift with no prediction made about where the pattern stands after a year is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Our clocks are set by the sun, not by the stars. While Earth spins, it is also moving along its orbit, so after one full spin Earth has to turn about one extra degree to bring the sun back due south, which takes about four minutes. The clock day is defined to include that extra turn, so the sun keeps time with it and the stars run four minutes fast every day. Partial: saying our clocks follow the sun, with nothing said about Earth having to turn an extra degree to bring the sun back due south, is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","Gravity, with its strength set by the mass of the sun, the mass of Earth and the distance between them. If the sun held more mass, its pull at Earth's distance would be stronger, Earth would have to travel faster to stay on a path of the same size, and the year would be shorter. A less massive sun would give a longer year. Partial: naming gravity with nothing said about a more massive sun forcing Earth to travel faster is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]},{std:["MS-ESS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"laying out the solar system at scale and reading an overturned sequence of dated layers",html:()=>`<p class="big">The town is marking a solar system along the 300 m lantern line, with the sun at one end and Neptune's lamp at the other.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Planet</th><th>Distance from the sun in AU</th></tr>
     <tr><td>Mercury</td><td>0.39</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Earth</td><td>1.00</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Jupiter</td><td>5.20</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Saturn</td><td>9.58</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Uranus</td><td>19.20</td></tr>
     <tr><td>Neptune</td><td>30.10</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The riverbank cut again, dated. Two kilometres upstream the same four units appear with the oldest on top.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Unit, top to bottom at the town cut</th><th>Age of the rock</th></tr>
     <tr><td>river sand</td><td>4,000 years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>clay</td><td>68 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>sandstone</td><td>150 million years</td></tr>
     <tr><td>mudstone</td><td>240 million years</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("globe",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Neptune's lamp stands at 300 m. Work out where Earth, Jupiter and Uranus go.","On that same scale Earth would be 0.9 mm across and the sun 9 cm. Say what the lantern line gets right and what it cannot show at all.","Two kilometres upstream the same four units sit with the oldest on top. Explain how that happens, and say which evidence you would trust to tell the true order."],key:["Neptune at 30.10 AU stands at 300 m, so 1 AU is 300 divided by 30.10, which is about 9.97 m. Earth goes at about 10.0 m, Jupiter at 5.20 times 9.97, about 51.8 m, and Uranus at 19.20 times 9.97, about 191.4 m. More than half the line is taken up by the gap between Uranus and Neptune alone. Partial: working out the 9.97 m for 1 AU with one or two of the three positions not placed is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","It gets the distances right relative to each other, and it shows how empty the outer solar system is: four planets crowd into the first 52 m and two take the remaining 250 m. It cannot show sizes on the same scale, because a 9 cm sun and a 0.9 mm Earth would be invisible beside 300 m of lamp posts. It also cannot show that the planets are moving, each at its own speed, or that the orbits are not a straight line. Partial: saying the line shows the distances with nothing named that the model cannot show is approaching. (MS-ESS1)","The beds were folded over, or pushed over a fault, so one limb of the fold is upside down and the oldest unit now lies on top. Position alone only gives the order where the beds have not been disturbed. The radiometric dates and the fossils inside each unit travel with the rock whatever happens to it afterward, so those are what tell the true order, and here they say the 240 million year mudstone formed first no matter which end of the stack it is at. Partial: saying the beds were overturned with nothing said about the dates and fossils travelling with the rock is approaching. (MS-ESS1)"]}]},{id:28,title:"Paper the Town Uses",short:"Human Impact",parts:[{std:["MS-ESS3"],n:3,dur:95,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"separating population growth from per person consumption in total demand on a resource",html:()=>`<p class="big">Paper and cardboard in this town, from the collection records. The recycling plant can take 2,400 tonnes a year; the rest is burned or buried.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Year</th><th>People</th><th>kg per person per year</th><th>Total</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1995</td><td>18,000</td><td>96</td><td>1,728 tonnes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2005</td><td>24,000</td><td>128</td><td>3,072 tonnes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2015</td><td>30,000</td><td>110</td><td>3,300 tonnes</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2025</td><td>36,000</td><td>82</td><td>2,952 tonnes</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("books",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("tree",74)}</div>`,qs:["This is a chart where the total went down at the end. Work out which of the two factors turned it, and by how much each moved.","Work out the per person figure the town would need to bring the total within the plant's 2,400 tonnes, and how many people the plant could serve at the 2025 rate.","Name two effects on Earth's systems of a larger total paper demand, and say what the chart shows about the limits of asking people to use less."],key:["Between 2015 and 2025 the number of people rose from 30,000 to 36,000, a rise of 20 percent, while per person use fell from 110 kg to 82 kg, a drop of about 25 percent. The 25 percent drop outweighed the 20 percent rise, so the product fell from 3,300 to 2,952 tonnes. Per person use is what turned the total, and it had to fall by more than the population grew in order to do it. Partial: naming per person use as what turned the total with the 20 percent and 25 percent not worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","2,400 tonnes is 2,400,000 kg, and divided among 36,000 people that is about 67 kg each, a further cut of about 18 percent below the 82 kg already reached. At the 2025 rate of 82 kg the plant could serve 2,400,000 divided by 82, which is about 29,000 people, so the town has already outgrown its plant by about 7,000 people. Partial: one of the two figures worked out is approaching. (MS-ESS3)","Any two: trees are cut faster than they regrow, which changes the forest and the soil, water and wildlife that depend on it; pulping and papermaking draw heavily on water and energy and release waste into both; and paper that is buried takes up landfill space and releases methane as it breaks down. The chart shows asking people to use less does work, since 82 kg is well below 128 kg, but it is a shrinking lever: it cannot go below what people actually need, while the population column has kept rising in every row. Partial: two effects named with nothing said about per person use being a lever that cannot go below what people need is approaching. (MS-ESS3)"]}]},{id:29,title:"Carts on the Lamp Line",short:"Forces",parts:[{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"applying the third law to a push between two objects and relating force and mass to motion",html:()=>`<p class="big">Two carts stand on the rails outside the depot with a squeezed spring between them. The spring is let go.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Cart</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed just after release</th></tr>
     <tr><td>cart of new lamps</td><td>90 kg</td><td>0.8 m/s north</td></tr>
     <tr><td>cart of old lamps</td><td>120 kg</td><td>0.6 m/s south</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">One lamp housing is then pushed along the bench with a measured force.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Force</th><th>Mass</th><th>Acceleration</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>24 N</td><td>8 kg</td><td>3 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>24 N</td><td>24 kg</td><td>1 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>72 N</td><td>8 kg</td><td>9 m/s each second</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>60 N</td><td>12 kg</td><td>5 m/s each second</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("box",74)}${ico("truck",74)}</div>`,qs:["One spring pushed both carts. Say what each cart experienced, using Newton's third law, and compare the two forces.","Multiply mass by speed for each cart. Say what the two products show. Then work out what the old lamp cart's speed would have been if the new lamp cart had massed 60 kg and still left at 0.8 m/s.","Which two trials differ only in the mass, and which two only in the force? Say what each pair establishes, then check trial 4 arithmetically."],key:["The spring pushed north on one cart and south on the other with forces equal in size and opposite in direction, at the same moment and for the same length of time. Neither cart is the active one; the pair of forces exists as one event. Both carts are free to roll, so both move away from the spring. Partial: saying both carts were pushed apart, with nothing said about the two forces being equal in size and opposite in direction, is approaching. (MS-PS2)","New lamps: 90 times 0.8 is 72. Old lamps: 120 times 0.6 is also 72. Equal and opposite quantities of motion, which is what a pair of equal and opposite forces acting for the same time always produces. A 60 kg cart at 0.8 m/s carries 48, so the 120 kg cart would leave at 48 divided by 120, which is 0.4 m/s south. Partial: finding both products equal to 72 with the 0.4 m/s prediction not worked out is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Trials 1 and 2 differ only in mass, 8 kg against 24 kg at 24 N, and the acceleration falls from 3 to 1, so three times the mass gives a third of the acceleration. Trials 1 and 3 differ only in force, 24 N against 72 N at 8 kg, and the acceleration rises from 3 to 9, so three times the force gives three times the acceleration. Trial 4 checks out: 12 kg times 5 m/s each second is 60 N. Partial: naming both pairs of trials with trial 4 not checked against force equals mass times acceleration is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]},{std:["MS-PS2"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Use all three charts. Answer out loud.",desc:"identifying what changes electric and magnetic forces, naming fields, and relating weight to mass",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp coil is wired as an electromagnet and used to lift a steel plate.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Turns of wire</th><th>Current</th><th>Core</th><th>Lift</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>80</td><td>2 A</td><td>soft iron</td><td>15 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>160</td><td>2 A</td><td>soft iron</td><td>29 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>80</td><td>4 A</td><td>soft iron</td><td>30 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>80</td><td>2 A</td><td>brass</td><td>2 N</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">A charged rod is held beside a thin stream of water from the tap, and the sideways bend is measured.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Rod</th><th>Bend at 3 cm</th><th>Bend at 6 cm</th></tr>
     <tr><td>rubbed 5 times</td><td>4 mm</td><td>1 mm</td></tr>
     <tr><td>rubbed 15 times</td><td>13 mm</td><td>3 mm</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">The same 30 kg lamp housing, weighed in three places.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Where</th><th>Weight on the scale</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the town square</td><td>294 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>on the moon</td><td>49 N</td></tr>
     <tr><td>at Jupiter's cloud tops</td><td>744 N</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("key",74)}${ico("drop",74)}${ico("globe",74)}</div>`,qs:["Use the first chart. Name the three factors that change the lift and give the trial pair that shows each one.","Use the second chart. Describe both patterns in it, then name what the distance pattern has in common with the magnet and with gravity.","Explain all three lamp housing weights, and name what crosses the gap in each of the three tests."],key:["Turns of wire: trials 1 and 2, where doubling them from 80 to 160 takes the lift from 15 N to 29 N. Current: trials 1 and 3, where doubling it from 2 A to 4 A takes the lift from 15 N to 30 N. The core material: trials 1 and 4, where swapping soft iron for brass drops the lift from 15 N to 2 N. The core is the largest effect, which makes sense because brass is not magnetic and contributes nothing. Partial: all three factors named with trial pairs but no judgment given on which effect is the largest is approaching. (MS-PS2)","Charge: at a fixed distance, trebling the rubbing from 5 to 15 strokes roughly treble the bend, 4 mm to 13 mm at 3 cm and 1 mm to 3 mm at 6 cm. Distance: at a fixed charge, doubling the distance from 3 cm to 6 cm cuts the bend to about a quarter, 4 mm to 1 mm and 13 mm to 3 mm. That quartering for a doubling is the same inverse square pattern the magnet's pull follows and the same one gravity follows, which is a reason to think all three act through a field spreading out from the source. Partial: describing both patterns with nothing said about the quartering for a doubling being the same pattern the magnet and gravity follow is approaching. (MS-PS2)","The housing holds the same 30 kg of matter everywhere. Weight is the gravitational force on it, set by its mass, the mass of the world, and the distance to that world's center. The moon's small mass gives 49 N, Earth gives 294 N, and Jupiter's enormous mass gives 744 N at its cloud tops. The gaps are filled by fields: a magnetic field between the coil and the plate, an electric field between the rod and the water, and a gravitational field between the housing and each world. Partial: explaining two of the three weights, or all three with no field named for the gaps, is approaching. (MS-PS2)"]}]},{id:30,title:"Up the Lamp Post",short:"Energy",parts:[{std:["MS-PS3"],n:4,dur:110,dir:"Use both charts. Work on your paper and tell your teacher each answer.",desc:"relating kinetic energy to mass and speed and stored energy to the arrangement of objects",html:()=>`<p class="big">A loaded cart's kinetic energy was measured on the flat outside the depot.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Trial</th><th>Mass</th><th>Speed</th><th>Kinetic energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>8 kg</td><td>5 m/s</td><td>100 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>16 kg</td><td>5 m/s</td><td>200 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>8 kg</td><td>10 m/s</td><td>400 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>2 kg</td><td>10 m/s</td><td>100 J</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Stored energy measured for a 30 kg lamp housing hoisted up a post, and for the wound spring in its counterweight.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Arrangement</th><th>Stored energy</th></tr>
     <tr><td>housing 2 m up the post</td><td>588 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>housing 5 m up the post</td><td>1,470 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>housing 8 m up the post</td><td>2,352 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>counterweight spring wound 2 turns</td><td>1.6 J</td></tr>
     <tr><td>counterweight spring wound 6 turns</td><td>14.4 J</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lantern",74)}${ico("key",74)}</div>`,qs:["Trials 1 and 4 both come to 100 J, and one has four times the mass of the other. Explain how they tie.","Use trials 1, 2 and 3 to state the rule for mass and the rule for speed, then work out the energy of a 16 kg cart at 10 m/s.","Use the lamp housing rows. State the rule, and explain what the 588 J is measured from and why that has to be said out loud.","The wound spring's energy rises nine times when the turns treble, while the housing's rises only three times when the height trebles. Explain why the two rules are not the same."],key:["Trial 4 has a quarter of trial 1's mass, which on its own would cut the energy to a quarter, and twice the speed, which on its own would multiply it by four. A quarter times four is one, so the two changes cancel exactly and both read 100 J. That only works because speed is squared and mass is not. Partial: noting that the two changes cancel with nothing said about speed being squared while mass is not is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Mass: trials 1 and 2 double the mass at the same 5 m/s and the energy doubles, 100 J to 200 J, so energy is proportional to mass. Speed: trials 1 and 3 double the speed at the same 8 kg and the energy quadruples, 100 J to 400 J, so energy goes up with the square of the speed. A 16 kg cart at 10 m/s doubles the mass and doubles the speed from trial 1, so 100 J times 2 times 4, which is 800 J. Partial: both rules stated with the 800 J not worked out, or a figure of 400 J that applies only one of the two changes, is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Stored energy is proportional to the height: 2 m gives 588 J, 5 m gives two and a half times that at 1,470 J, and 8 m gives four times it at 2,352 J. The 588 J is measured from the foot of the post, which is a choice somebody made. Measured from the cellar floor below, or from the top of the post, the same housing at the same place would carry a different number, because stored energy belongs to the arrangement of the housing and Earth and needs a reference position named before it has a value. Partial: stating the proportion to height with nothing said about the foot of the post being a chosen reference is approaching. (MS-PS3)","Earth pulls the housing with practically the same force at every height, so every extra metre of lift stores the same amount and the total is simply proportional to the height. The spring pushes back harder the more it is wound, so each extra turn stores more than the turn before it, and the total goes up with the square of the turns. The rule depends on how the force behaves as the parts move, which is why a rule found for one store has to be measured again for another. Partial: noting that the two rules differ with nothing said about the spring pushing back harder the more it is wound is approaching. (MS-PS3)"]}]},{id:31,title:"The Bell in the Square",short:"Waves",parts:[{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Look at the chart. Work on your paper and answer out loud.",desc:"relating amplitude to the energy in a wave and using wavelength and frequency to find wave speed",html:()=>`<p class="big">The bell in the square was struck four ways and a sensor 50 m off recorded the energy arriving.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Strike</th><th>How far the clapper was drawn back</th><th>Wavelength</th><th>Cycles per second</th><th>Energy at the sensor</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A</td><td>3 cm</td><td>0.68 m</td><td>500</td><td>1 unit</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B</td><td>6 cm</td><td>0.68 m</td><td>500</td><td>4 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C</td><td>12 cm</td><td>0.68 m</td><td>500</td><td>16 units</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D</td><td>12 cm</td><td>1.36 m</td><td>250</td><td>16 units</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("megaphone",74)}${ico("clock",74)}</div>`,qs:["The clapper's draw doubles from A to B and doubles again to C. Say what the energy does at each step and name the rule.","Strike D sounds an octave lower than strike C. Work out the speed of the sound from both rows and say why they agree.","Someone in the square says a louder bell travels faster. Use the chart to answer."],key:["Each doubling of the draw multiplies the energy by four: 1 unit at 3 cm, 4 units at 6 cm, 16 units at 12 cm. The energy goes up with the square of the amplitude, so four times the draw gives sixteen times the energy, and amplitude is the property of a wave that carries its energy. Partial: saying a harder strike is louder, with nothing said about the energy going up with the square of the amplitude, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","Speed is wavelength times cycles per second. C: 0.68 m times 500 is 340 m per second. D: 1.36 m times 250 is also 340 m per second. The speed of sound is set by the air, not by the bell, so when the wavelength doubled the frequency had to halve. That is also why D is an octave lower: an octave down is exactly half the frequency. Partial: working out one of the two speeds, or both with nothing said about the air rather than the bell setting the speed, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The chart says no. Strikes A, B and C deliver 1, 4 and 16 units of energy, which is as loud as the bell gets, and all three have the same 0.68 m wavelength and 500 cycles per second, so all three travel at 340 m per second. Loudness is amplitude and tells you how much energy arrives; speed is set by the medium. A louder bell reaches further before fading below hearing, which can feel like arriving sooner, but it does not arrive sooner. Partial: answering no with nothing said about all three strikes sharing the same wavelength and frequency is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]},{std:["MS-PS4"],n:3,dur:80,dir:"Use the chart and the two methods. Answer out loud.",desc:"reading reflection, absorption and transmission from data and comparing a digital with an analog signal",html:()=>`<p class="big">A lamp sends 100 units of light at each material. One meter sits behind it and one beside the lamp.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Material</th><th>Passed through</th><th>Came back</th><th>Neither</th></tr>
     <tr><td>the lamp's clear glass</td><td>92</td><td>6</td><td>2</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the lamp's frosted globe</td><td>71</td><td>17</td><td>12</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a white painted reflector</td><td>1</td><td>84</td><td>15</td></tr>
     <tr><td>the same reflector, blackened with soot</td><td>0</td><td>11</td><td>89</td></tr>
     <tr><td>a sheet of thin paper</td><td>46</td><td>38</td><td>16</td></tr></table>
     <p class="big">Two ways for the crews to send each night's count of lamps replaced down one wire to the depot.<br>Method 1: a meter needle whose position stands for the count.<br>Method 2: the count sent as a string of on and off pulses.</p>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("phone",74)}</div>`,qs:["The white reflector sends back 84 and the sooty one 11. Say where the light goes in each case and what that means for the light the lamp puts on the street.","Both the clear glass and the thin paper let light through. Say which you could read a page through and why the chart tells you so.","The wire picks up interference from a motor in the next building. Say which method still reports the right count and why, then name what has to be agreed before that method works at all."],key:["The white reflector sends 84 of 100 back out of the lamp and down to the street, taking up only 15. The sooty one absorbs 89 and sends back 11, so almost all of that light turns into a slight warming of the soot and never reaches the pavement. A clean reflector is worth most of the bulb: the lamp can put out the same light and deliver seven or eight times as much of it where it is wanted. Partial: naming absorption as where the sooty reflector's light goes with nothing said about what that costs the street is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The clear glass. It passes 92 of 100 and passes it straight through, so the pattern of light leaving matches the pattern arriving and the print stays readable. The paper passes 46 but it scatters what it passes in every direction, which is why it also sends 38 back, so the pattern is destroyed and a page behind it is a blur. Seeing through something takes light that passes and keeps its direction, not just light that passes. Partial: choosing the glass on the 92 against the 46 alone, with nothing said about the paper scattering what it passes, is approaching. (MS-PS4)","The string of pulses. It is a digital signal: each pulse only has to be told apart from no pulse, so noise that would nudge a needle leaves on and off perfectly distinguishable and the count arrives exactly as sent. The needle is analog, where the value is the needle's position, so interference moves the needle and the depot writes down a wrong count with nothing to warn it. What has to be agreed first is the code: both ends must use the same pulse length, the same grouping and the same meaning for each group, or the pulses arrive perfectly and mean nothing. Partial: choosing the pulses with nothing said about why the needle hides its own error, or with nothing said about the code having to be agreed first, is approaching. (MS-PS4)"]}]},{id:32,title:"Forty Lamps to Replace",short:"Design",parts:[{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Read the situation and the chart. Answer out loud.",desc:"defining a design problem with criteria and constraints and evaluating competing designs against them",html:()=>`<p class="big">Forty of the town's street lamps have to be replaced before the long nights end. The town has $6,000, three weeks of one crew's time, a rule that no lamp may shine into a bedroom window, and no budget for new underground cable. The council has asked for at least 15 lux on the pavement.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Design</th><th>Cost for 40</th><th>Crew time</th><th>Light on the pavement</th><th>Shines into windows</th><th>Needs new cable</th></tr>
     <tr><td>A: same lamp, new bulb</td><td>$1,200</td><td>4 days</td><td>9 lux</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>B: brighter lamp on the same post</td><td>$4,400</td><td>9 days</td><td>26 lux</td><td>yes, above the second floor</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>C: shielded lamp on the same post</td><td>$5,200</td><td>12 days</td><td>21 lux</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
     <tr><td>D: new low posts along the kerb</td><td>$9,800</td><td>18 days</td><td>23 lux</td><td>no</td><td>yes</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lantern",74)}${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Write out the problem, then list the criteria and the constraints, and say where the 15 lux figure belongs.","Name every design that fails and say what each one fails on.","Defend the design that is left using the chart, and name one thing you would check before ordering forty of them."],key:["Achieve: light the pavement under forty street lamps well enough to walk by, without lighting bedrooms, before the long nights end. Criterion, the measure of success: light on the pavement, with 15 lux as the level the council set, so the 15 lux figure is the threshold attached to the criterion rather than a constraint on the solution. Constraints, the limits on any solution: no more than $6,000, no more than three weeks of one crew, no light into bedroom windows, and no new underground cable. Partial: a goal stated with the 15 lux filed as a constraint rather than as the threshold on the criterion is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Design A fails the criterion: 9 lux against the 15 lux asked for. Design B fails a constraint: it shines into windows above the second floor. Design D fails two constraints: $9,800 against a $6,000 budget, and it needs new cable. Only design C fails nothing. Partial: two or three of the four designs judged correctly is approaching, and D's two separate failures are usually counted as one. (MS-ETS1)","Design C: 21 lux clears the 15 lux the council set, it keeps light out of windows, it needs no new cable, and at $5,200 and 12 days it fits inside $6,000 and three weeks. It is the only one that fails nothing, though with $800 and little time to spare. Before ordering forty, check what 21 lux measures as on the town's own pavements rather than in the supplier's test, since a wet dark surface returns far less light than a pale dry one, and the whole choice turns on that single figure. Partial: defending Design C from the chart with nothing named that would be checked before ordering forty is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]},{std:["MS-ETS1"],n:3,dur:85,dir:"Look at the test results. Answer out loud.",desc:"analyzing test data to find which change helps and planning the next round of testing",html:()=>`<p class="big">Four versions of the shielded lamp were tested on the same stretch of pavement. The second measure is how much of the lamp's light goes out above the horizontal, which is the light that reaches windows.</p>
     <table class="bigTable"><tr><th>Version</th><th>Shield</th><th>Lamp height</th><th>Bulb</th><th>Light on the pavement</th><th>Light above the horizontal</th></tr>
     <tr><td>1</td><td>none</td><td>5 m</td><td>40 W</td><td>24 lux</td><td>31 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>2</td><td>tilted 20 degrees</td><td>5 m</td><td>40 W</td><td>22 lux</td><td>9 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>3</td><td>tilted 20 degrees</td><td>5 m</td><td>60 W</td><td>33 lux</td><td>13 percent</td></tr>
     <tr><td>4</td><td>tilted 20 degrees</td><td>7 m</td><td>40 W</td><td>16 lux</td><td>6 percent</td></tr></table>
     <div class="figure">${ico("lamp",74)}${ico("ruler",74)}</div>`,qs:["Name what changed between versions 1 and 2 and what it cost, then name what changed between 2 and 4 and what that cost.","Version 3 raised the bulb power. Say what it bought and what it gave back, and judge it against the town's two aims.","Specify a fifth version, predict both of its numbers, and name the variable the four runs have not separated."],key:["Versions 1 to 2: the shield was added, and the light going above the horizontal fell from 31 percent to 9 percent, which is the whole point, at a cost of 2 lux on the pavement, 24 down to 22. Versions 2 to 4: the lamp was raised from 5 m to 7 m, and the light above the horizontal fell further, 9 percent to 6 percent, at a cost of 6 lux, 22 down to 16, which drops it to the edge of the 15 lux the council asked for. Partial: naming both changes with the cost in lux not given for one of them is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","The bigger bulb bought 11 lux on the pavement, 22 up to 33, and gave back some of the shielding gain: light above the horizontal rose from 9 percent to 13 percent, because a brighter source spills more through the same shield. Against the town's two aims it is a clear gain on pavement light and a clear loss on window light, and it also costs more to run every night. It is worth taking only if 13 percent is still low enough to keep light out of windows, which the chart does not say. Partial: naming the 11 lux gained with nothing said about the spill rising from 9 to 13 percent is approaching. (MS-ETS1)","Any version argued from the chart. The strongest is a 60 W bulb at 5 m behind a deeper shield, since the bulb is the only change that raised pavement light and a deeper shield is the only way tested to cut the spill; predict well over 25 lux with the spill back near 9 percent or below. The variable the four runs never separated is the shield angle itself: 20 degrees was used in three versions and never varied, so the next test should hold height at 5 m and the bulb at 60 W and compare 10, 20, 30 and 40 degrees to find the angle that gives the most pavement light for the least spill. Partial: a fifth version argued from the chart with the shield angle not identified as the variable never varied is approaching. (MS-ETS1)"]}]}];const LEVELUPS={1:{id:"L1",title:"Level Up: Strong and Thorough",std:["RL.9-10.1"],needs:["RL.8.1"],n:1,dir:"Answer out loud. Read me the exact words for each detail.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Claim: Elias comes to the substation for his granddaughter and for the line, not for the town. Prove it with strong and thorough evidence. Give at least three details from different parts of the story, and explain what each one shows.</p>${story()}`,key:["Three or more details from across the story, each explained. Examples: he “had not said yes” when the town told him to be there, so the council’s invitation did not move him. He came “at five of,” after the councilors and the photographer were already in place, so he arrived for the moment and not the gathering. “He did not look at the camera,” which refuses the photograph the council wanted. “He stood where he could see the street,” facing the thing he built rather than the ceremony. He “put one hand flat on the housing of the old cabinet, the way you would put a hand on a horse,” which is a goodbye to the equipment. And “Go on,” which is spoken to Nadia alone and hands the moment to her. (HS RL.9-10.1, strong and thorough textual evidence)"]},2:{id:"L2",title:"Level Up: Eliminate Both Ways",std:["A-REI.6"],needs:["8.EE.C.8"],n:2,dir:"Solve on your paper and hold it up. Show your steps and check each answer in both equations.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Solve this pair: 2x + 5y = 1 and 3x − 2y = 30.</li><li>Solve this pair: 4x + 3y = 27 and 6x + 5y = 43.</li></ol>`,key:["x = 8 and y = −3, or (8, −3). Steps: multiply the first equation by 2 to get 4x + 10y = 2, and the second by 5 to get 15x − 10y = 150. Add them: 19x = 152, so x = 8. Then 2(8) + 5y = 1 gives 5y = −15 and y = −3. Check in the second: 3(8) − 2(−3) = 24 + 6 = 30. (HS A-REI.6)","x = 3 and y = 5, or (3, 5). Steps: multiply the first equation by 3 to get 12x + 9y = 81, and the second by 2 to get 12x + 10y = 86. Subtract: y = 5. Then 4x + 3(5) = 27 gives 4x = 12 and x = 3. Check in the second: 6(3) + 5(5) = 18 + 25 = 43. Both equations had to be multiplied. (HS A-REI.6)"]},3:{id:"L3",title:"Level Up: Two Wants at Once",std:["RL.9-10.3"],needs:["RL.8.3"],n:2,dir:"Answer out loud. Use details from the story.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Elias wants two things that pull against each other. Name both, and give a detail from the story for each one.</li><li>How does his last line show the way he develops across the scene? What idea about the town does that development bring out?</li></ol>${story()}`,key:["He wants his forty-one years of work to count as work that was asked for and done well: “I wired that line when there was nothing on this street at six o’clock but one bulb at the mill gate,” and “People could see the sky fine. They wanted the street,” and he calls the night “an inventory” rather than a tribute. He also wants to be there for Nadia and for the line’s last moment: he had “not said yes” but “came at five of,” put a hand on the old cabinet, and told her “Go on.” Needs both wants and a detail for each. (HS RL.9-10.3, a complex character with conflicting motivations)","“Well. Look at that” is the first thing he says that is not a defense of the old line. He has moved from refusing the ceremony to standing in the street and looking, first up the street and then up at the sky, and the story leaves which one he meant unsettled. That brings out the idea that the town is not correcting a mistake but changing its mind about what it wants from a night, and that the person who built the old answer can look at the new one without either of them being wrong. (HS RL.9-10.3, how a character develops and advances the theme)"]},6:{id:"L6",title:"Level Up: Same Step or Same Ratio",std:["F-LE.1"],needs:["8.F.A.3"],n:3,dir:"Work on your paper. Tell your teacher each answer and how you decided.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A town starts with 420 unshielded lamps and replaces 40 of them every year. Is the number left a linear function or an exponential function of the number of years? Write the rule.</li><li>Each sheet of waxed paper lets through half of the light that reaches it. After n sheets, the fraction of the light still getting through is (1/2)<sup>n</sup>. Linear or exponential? How do you know?</li><li>This table shows the brightness of a lamp through a stack of filters. Is it linear or exponential? Write the rule.</li></ol>${bigTable(["Number of filters, t","0","1","2","3"],[["Brightness, v","64","48","36","27"]])}`,key:["Linear. The same amount, 40 lamps, is taken away in equal steps, and a linear function is the one with equal differences over equal intervals. The rule is L = 420 − 40y. (HS F-LE.1a and F-LE.1b)","Exponential. Each sheet multiplies the light by the same factor, 1/2, rather than taking away the same amount, and a function that changes by a constant ratio over equal intervals is exponential. (HS F-LE.1a and F-LE.1c)","Exponential, with the rule v = 64(3/4)<sup>t</sup>. The differences are not constant: 48 − 64 = −16, 36 − 48 = −12, and 27 − 36 = −9. The ratios are constant: 48 ÷ 64 = 0.75, 36 ÷ 48 = 0.75, and 27 ÷ 36 = 0.75. (HS F-LE.1)"]},8:{id:"L8",title:"Level Up: Angles on the Wire",std:["G-SRT.8"],needs:["8.G.B.7"],n:2,dir:"Work on your paper with a calculator. Show the ratio you used and hold it up.",mats:["a calculator"],html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>A guy wire meets the ground at an angle of 62° and its stake is 14 ft from the base of the lamp post. How tall is the post, to the nearest tenth of a foot?</li><li>A 30 ft guy wire runs from the top of a 24 ft post down to a stake. What angle does the wire make with the ground, to the nearest degree? Then use the Pythagorean theorem to find how far the stake is from the base.</li></ol>`,key:["About 26.3 ft. The post is opposite the 62° angle and the 14 ft is next to it, so tan 62° = height ÷ 14, which gives height = 14 × tan 62° = 14 × 1.8807 = 26.33, or 26.3 ft. (HS G-SRT.8, a trigonometric ratio in an applied problem)","About 53°, and the stake is 18 ft from the base. The post is opposite the angle and the wire is the hypotenuse, so sin θ = 24 ÷ 30 = 0.8, and θ = 53.13°, which rounds to 53°. Then 30<sup>2</sup> − 24<sup>2</sup> = 900 − 576 = 324, and the square root of 324 is 18. Check: cos 53.13° = 18 ÷ 30 = 0.6. (HS G-SRT.8, a trigonometric ratio and the Pythagorean theorem together)"]},11:{id:"L11",title:"Level Up: Phrase It Together",std:["L.9-10.1"],needs:["L.8.1"],n:3,dir:"Say each new sentence out loud, then name the phrase or clause you used.",html:()=>`<ol class="qlist"><li>Combine these into one sentence using a participial phrase: “The old cabinet stood open at the substation. It showed forty-one years of splices.”</li><li>Combine these into one sentence using a subordinate clause that begins with Because: “The caps had been removed from fifteen fixtures. The lot was running unshielded.”</li><li>Combine these into one sentence using an appositive phrase: “Elias Obeng put a hand on the cabinet. He is the man who wired the line in 1961.”</li></ol>`,key:["“Standing open at the substation, the old cabinet showed forty-one years of splices.” Names the participial phrase: Standing open at the substation. Accept any correct version built on a participial phrase. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)","“Because the caps had been removed from fifteen fixtures, the lot was running unshielded.” Names it as a subordinate clause. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)","“Elias Obeng, the man who wired the line in 1961, put a hand on the cabinet.” Names the appositive phrase: the man who wired the line in 1961. (HS L.9-10.1, various types of phrases and clauses)"]},12:{id:"L12",title:"Level Up: Hold the Line",std:["W.9-10.1"],needs:["W.8.1"],n:2,dir:"Add to the writing on your paper, or say it out loud.",html:()=>`<p class="big">Add two or three sentences to the argument you just wrote.</p><ol class="qlist"><li>State the strongest version of the opposing claim, using a real number from the fact box, and then point out what that side leaves out.</li><li>Rewrite your closing so that it follows from your reasoning and keeps a formal, objective tone with no second person.</li></ol>`,key:["States the opposing claim at its strongest, with a number from the fact box rather than a weakened version, and then names what that side leaves out, such as the 2029 date on one side or the six walkers on the other. (HS W.9-10.1a and W.9-10.1b, developing claims and counterclaims fairly, pointing out the strengths and limits of both)","The new closing follows from the argument the student actually made, supports it rather than repeating the claim, and keeps a formal, objective tone with no slang and no address to the reader. (HS W.9-10.1d and W.9-10.1e, formal objective tone and conclusion)"]}};const READ_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","RL.9-10.1","RL.9-10.3"];const WRITE_STD=["W.8.1","W.8.2","W.8.3","W.9-10.1"];const MATH_STD=["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.EE.C.8","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1","A-REI.6","F-LE.1","G-SRT.8"];const TEXT_STD=["RL.8.1","RL.8.3","RL.8.4","RI.8.1","RI.8.8","RI.8.9","W.8.8","RL.9-10.1","RL.9-10.3"];const LOG_NAMES={readq:"Questions read aloud",passage:"Text read aloud by an adult",repeat:"Directions repeated or reworded",vocab:"Word or phrase explained by teacher",hint:"Hint or first step given",fewer:"Fewer questions",time:"Extra time",scribe:"Adult wrote the student’s words",brk:"Break"};function stopLogs(st){const out=["readq"];if(READ_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("passage");out.push("repeat","vocab","hint","fewer","time");if(WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8"]).some(x=>st.std.includes(x)))out.push("scribe");out.push("brk");return out}const dl=a=>`<dl>${a.map(x=>`<dt>${x[0]}</dt><dd>${x[1]}</dd>`).join("")}</dl>`;const ul=a=>`<ul class="card-list">${a.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`;function wt(st){return st.std.includes("W.8.8")?"notes":st.std.includes("SL.8.4")?"say":st.std.includes("W.8.3")?"narrative":st.std.includes("W.8.2")?"explain":"argument"}function twoCol(st){return st.id===10}function stopSpeak(){try{window.speechSynthesis&&window.speechSynthesis.cancel()}catch(e){}}function guardSpeech(){["mapBtn","teacherBtn","newBtn","prevBtn","gemBtn","skipBtn"].forEach(i=>{const el=document.getElementById(i);if(el&&!el.dataset.tts){el.dataset.tts="1";el.addEventListener("click",stopSpeak)}})}function speakText(t){stopSpeak();guardSpeech();try{const u=new SpeechSynthesisUtterance(t);u.rate=+(document.getElementById("ttsRate")||{value:.9}).value;window.speechSynthesis.speak(u)}catch(e){}}function textOf(sel){return[...document.querySelectorAll(sel)].map(e=>e.textContent.trim()).join(" ")}function listenHtml(st){const two=twoCol(st);return`<div class="blk-row">${two?`<button class="chip" data-say="a">Play Text X</button><button class="chip" data-say="b">Play Text Y</button>`:`<button class="chip" data-say="p">Play the text</button>`}<button class="chip" data-say="q">Play the questions</button><button class="chip" data-say="x">Stop</button></div><label class="small">Speed <select id="ttsRate"><option value="0.75">Slow</option><option value="0.9" selected>Normal</option><option value="1.1">Fast</option></select></label><p class="small">Uses this computer’s voice. If you hear nothing, check the volume, then read the text aloud yourself and mark “Text read aloud by an adult.”</p>`}function listenBind(b){b.querySelectorAll("[data-say]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.say,card="#stopView .card ";if(k==="x")return stopSpeak();if(k==="p")speakText(textOf(card+".passage"));if(k==="a")speakText(textOf(card+".texts article:nth-child(1)"));if(k==="b")speakText(textOf(card+".texts article:nth-child(2)"));if(k==="q")speakText(textOf(card+"ol.qlist > li:not([hidden]), "+card+"> .big"))})}const G_STORY=[["substation","a small fenced building where the electricity for a neighborhood is switched and controlled"],["handle","here, the large lever that switches a whole circuit on or off"],["inventory","a count of everything a business owns, and what it is worth"],["mercury light","the old kind of street lamp, which gives a blue-white or orange cast"],["housing","the metal case around a machine"],["traces","not used here"],["grant","money given to a town to pay for a project"],["councilor","a member of the town council"]];const G_SKY=[["skyglow","the dim glow over a town, caused by stray light scattered in the air"],["scatter","to send light off in many directions"],["sky quality meter","an instrument pointed straight up that measures how bright the sky is"],["magnitude","a step on the brightness scale. One step is a change of about two and a half times."],["shielded or capped fixture","a lamp with a cap over the top so the light goes down instead of up"],["wavelength","what makes one color of light different from another. Blue is short, amber is long."],["ordinance","a local law passed by a town"]];const G_MEMO=[["memo","a short written message inside an organization"],["floodlight","a wide, very bright outdoor lamp"],["distribution warehouse","a large building where goods are stored and sent out"],["vacancy rate","the share of shops or offices sitting empty"],["sample","the group of people actually asked in a survey"],["parcel","one piece of land with a boundary"],["bay","one loading or working area of a warehouse"]];const G_SRC=[["credible","able to be trusted"],["plagiarism","using someone else’s words or ideas as if they were your own"],["paraphrase","to say it in your own words"],["citation","the line at the end of a report that says where a fact came from"],["glare","light shining straight into your eyes so that you see less, not more"],["obstacle","something in the way that a walker could trip on"],["trial","one run of a test in an experiment"]];const G_WRITE={argument:[["claim","what you want the reader to believe or do"],["counterclaim","the strongest thing the other side would say"],["relevant evidence","facts that actually bear on the claim"],["formal style","writing with no slang, no texting language, and no jokes to the reader"],["objective tone","sticking to the case, with no personal digs and no exclamations"]],narrative:[["point of view","who is telling the story"],["pacing","how fast or slow the story moves through its events"],["sensory language","words for what can be seen, heard, felt, smelled, or tasted"],["reflection","a sentence where the narrator says what the events meant"]],notes:[["search terms","the words you type to find sources"],["source","where a fact came from"],["quotation marks","the marks that show you are using someone’s exact words"]],say:[["recommendation","what you are telling the council to do"],["drawback","the cost or weakness of your own choice"],["fixed post","the one spot a measurement is always taken from, so readings can be compared"]]};function glossHtml(st){if(st.id===12)return dl(G_WRITE[wt(st)]||G_WRITE.argument);if(st.id===13)return dl(G_WRITE.notes.concat(G_SRC));if(st.id===14)return dl(G_WRITE.say.concat(G_SKY));if(st.id===7)return dl(G_SKY);if(st.id===9||st.id===10)return dl(G_MEMO.concat(G_SKY));return dl(G_STORY)}const TERMS=[["cite","point to the exact words in the text that prove your answer"],["strongest evidence","the words that prove the answer best, not just words on the same topic"],["explicit","stated right in the text"],["inference","something the text does not say outright, but that you can figure out from clues"],["connotation","the feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning"],["simile","a comparison using like or as, or a phrase such as the way you would"],["dialogue","the words characters say out loud"],["incident","one event in a story"],["propel the action","push the story forward so that something new has to happen"],["motivation","what a character wants, and why"],["claim","what a writer wants you to believe or do"],["relevant","actually about the claim"],["sufficient","enough to prove the point"],["sound reasoning","thinking that makes sense and follows from the evidence"],["fact or interpretation","a fact can be checked; an interpretation is what someone thinks the facts mean"],["thorough evidence","enough details, from more than one place in the text"]];const FRAMES={cite:["The answer is ____. The words that prove it are “____.”","The strongest evidence is “____” because ____.","I can infer that ____ because the text says “____.”"],"RL.8.3":["When ____ says “____,” it makes ____ decide to ____.","Because ____ happened, ____ now has to ____.","This line shows that ____ is ____ because ____."],"RL.8.4":["The word ____ suggests ____, while ____ suggests ____.","This is a ____ (simile, metaphor), and it makes the reader see ____.","The author chose ____ instead of ____, which has the effect of ____."],"RI.8.8":["Vasquez claims that ____.","One reason he gives is ____, backed by ____.","That evidence is relevant because ____ and sufficient because ____.","The sentence about ____ does not belong because ____.","This reasoning is weak because ____."],"RI.8.9":["Text X says ____, but Text Y says ____. That is a disagreement about a fact.","They both agree that ____, but Text X reads it as ____ while Text Y reads it as ____.","To settle the fact you would need ____.","No evidence settles the other one because ____."]};function framesHtml(st){const k=st.std.includes("RI.8.9")?"RI.8.9":st.std.includes("RI.8.8")?"RI.8.8":st.std.includes("RL.8.4")?"RL.8.4":st.std.includes("RL.8.3")||st.std.includes("RL.9-10.3")?"RL.8.3":"cite";return`<ul class="starters" style="font-size:1.2rem">${FRAMES[k].map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul>`}function gramHtml(){return`<h4>Verbals: verb forms doing another job</h4>`+dl([["Gerund","ends in -ing and acts as a noun. <em>Hanging lanterns is slow work.</em>"],["Participle","often ends in -ing or -ed and describes a noun. <em>The failing bulb flickered.</em>"],["Infinitive","to plus a verb. <em>She hoped to finish by nine.</em>"]])+`<h4>Voice</h4>`+dl([["Active","the subject does the action. <em>The crew opened the cabinet.</em>"],["Passive","the subject receives the action. <em>The cabinet was opened by the crew.</em>"]])+`<h4>Mood</h4>`+dl([["Indicative","states a fact. <em>The wind is rising.</em>"],["Imperative","gives a command. <em>Check the reading.</em>"],["Interrogative","asks a question. <em>Is the wind rising?</em>"],["Conditional","tells what would or could happen. <em>I would go if the sky were clear.</em>"],["Subjunctive","a wish or something not true. Uses were. <em>If the sky were darker, we could see it.</em>"]])+`<h4>Phrases and clauses</h4>`+dl([["Participial phrase","a participle plus its words, describing a noun. <em>Standing open, the cabinet showed its splices.</em>"],["Subordinate clause","has a subject and a verb but cannot stand alone. <em>Because the caps were gone, the lot ran unshielded.</em>"],["Appositive phrase","renames the noun right beside it. <em>Nadia, the lineman’s granddaughter, pulled the handle.</em>"]])+`<p class="small">Keep the same voice and the same mood all the way through a sentence.</p>`}const PLAN={explain:["My topic, and what I will cover","What the meter reads and what the numbers mean","Why the scale is not linear","What 19.1 to 19.9 actually is","What it still is not, and my closing line"],argument:["My claim","Reason 1 and the evidence from the fact box","Reason 2 and the evidence from the fact box","The other side's claim, and my answer to it","Conclusion"],narrative:["Who, where, and when","What happens at nine o'clock","What happened first, next, last","A line of dialogue I will use","How it ended and what it meant"],notes:["Which source I trust, and why","My search terms","My quote or paraphrase","My citation"],say:["My recommendation","My strongest evidence from the table","The drawback","What I need from the council"]};function planHtml(st){const S=E.state;S.plan=S.plan||{};const k=wt(st),v=S.plan[k]||[];return`<div class="planner">${PLAN[k].map((x,i)=>`<label>${x}<textarea data-pi="${i}">${(v[i]||"").replace(/</g,"&lt;")}</textarea></label>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Your plan is saved for the speaking stop and the writing stop.</p>`}function planBind(b,r,st){b.querySelectorAll("[data-pi]").forEach(t=>t.oninput=()=>{const S=E.state,k=wt(st);S.plan=S.plan||{};S.plan[k]=S.plan[k]||[];S.plan[k][+t.dataset.pi]=t.value;E.save()})}const SENT={explain:{s:["A sky quality meter reads ____.","On that scale, ____ means ____.","It is worth knowing that ____.","For example, Hollowell ____.","All the same, ____."],w:["because","for example","in other words","however","as a result","still","in short"]},argument:{s:["The council should fund ____ because ____.","The table shows that ____.","Some would argue that ____. However, ____.","This evidence matters because ____.","For these reasons, ____."],w:["because","for example","however","although","in addition","as a result","therefore","on the other hand"]},narrative:{s:["It was ____ when the street ____.","At first, ____.","“____,” ____ said.","A few seconds later, ____.","By the time ____, ____.","Looking back, I understood that ____."],w:["at first","a few seconds later","suddenly","after that","by the time","finally","looking back"]},notes:{s:["I trust Source ____ more because ____.","These search terms are best because ____.","Dr. Hannah Ozturk reports, “____.”","According to Dr. Hannah Ozturk, ____.","Author. “Title.” Publisher, year."],w:["according to","reports","states","explains"]},say:{s:["The council should fund ____.","My strongest evidence is ____.","The drawback is ____.","For that reason, ____.","From the council I need ____."],w:["because","the table shows","compared with","by contrast","for that reason"]}};function sentHtml(st){const d=SENT[wt(st)]||SENT.argument;return`<ul class="starters">${d.s.map(x=>`<li>${x}</li>`).join("")}</ul><h4>Linking words</h4><div class="wordbank">${d.w.map(x=>`<span>${x}</span>`).join("")}</div>`}const CHECK={explain:["I introduced the topic and said what I would cover.","I used facts and definitions from the box.","I got the direction of the scale right: a larger number is a darker sky.","I explained that the scale is not linear.","I used transitions.","I used precise words: magnitude, scale, reading, linear.","I kept a formal style.","I ended with a concluding statement.","I reread it for mistakes."],argument:["I stated my claim.","I named the other side and showed how my claim is different.","I gave reasons with evidence from the fact box.","I used the numbers accurately.","I used linking words.","I kept a formal, objective style.","I ended with a conclusion.","I reread it for mistakes."],narrative:["I set the scene.","Events are in an order that makes sense.","I used at least two lines of dialogue.","I used details the reader can see, hear, or feel.","I used time words.","My ending reflects on what happened.","I reread it for mistakes."]};function checkHtml(st){return`<ul class="card-list" style="list-style:none;padding-left:0">${(CHECK[wt(st)]||CHECK.argument).map(x=>`<li><label style="display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:flex-start"><input type="checkbox" style="width:20px;height:20px;margin-top:4px"> ${x}</label></li>`).join("")}</ul>`}let calcE="",calcAns="";function calcEval(){let e=calcE.replace(/×/g,"*").replace(/÷/g,"/").replace(/−/g,"-").replace(/\^/g,"**").replace(/√\(/g,"Math.sqrt(");if(!/^[0-9+\-*/().\sMathsqr]*$/.test(e)||!e)return"";try{const o=(e.match(/\(/g)||[]).length-(e.match(/\)/g)||[]).length;if(o>0)e+=")".repeat(o);const v=Function('"use strict";return ('+e+")")();return Number.isFinite(v)?String(+v.toPrecision(10)):"Error"}catch(x){return"Error"}}function calcHtml(){const keys=["7","8","9","÷","⌫","4","5","6","×","(","1","2","3","−",")","0",".","^","+","C","√(","x²","="];return`<div class="calc-disp" aria-live="polite">${calcE||"0"}${calcAns!==""?`<br><strong>= ${calcAns}</strong>`:""}</div><div class="calc-keys">${keys.map(k=>`<button data-k="${k}" class="${k==="="?"eq":/[0-9.]/.test(k)?"":"op"}">${k==="^"?"xʸ":k==="√("?"√":k}</button>`).join("")}</div><p class="small">Press √ then the number and close the bracket. For a square, press x². This calculator has no sine, cosine, or tangent key.</p>`}function calcBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-k]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const k=x.dataset.k;if(k==="C"){calcE="";calcAns=""}else if(k==="⌫"){calcE=calcE.replace(/(√\(|.)$/,"");calcAns=""}else if(k==="="){calcAns=calcEval()}else{if(calcAns!==""&&calcAns!=="Error"&&/^[+−×÷^x]/.test(k))calcE=calcAns;else if(calcAns!=="")calcE="";calcAns="";calcE+=k==="x²"?"^2":k}r()})}const MULT_N=12;let multSel=[0,0];function multHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>×</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++)h+=`<th data-r="0" data-c="${c}" class="${multSel[1]===c?"hl":""}">${c}</th>`;h+="</tr>";for(let r=1;r<=MULT_N;r++){h+=`<tr><th data-r="${r}" data-c="0" class="${multSel[0]===r?"hl":""}">${r}</th>`;for(let c=1;c<=MULT_N;c++){const hr=multSel[0]===r,hc=multSel[1]===c;h+=`<td data-r="${r}" data-c="${c}" class="${hr&&hc?"pick":hr||hc?"hl":""}">${r*c}</td>`}h+="</tr>"}return h+`</table></div><p class="small">Click a number to light up its row and column. Click it again to clear.</p>`}function sqHtml(){let h=`<div class="tool-scroll"><table class="mult"><tr><th>n</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=20;n++)h+=`<th>${n}</th>`;h+=`</tr><tr><th>n²</th>`;for(let n=1;n<=20;n++)h+=`<td>${n*n}</td>`;return h+`</tr></table></div><p class="small">n² means n × n. To find a square root, find the number in the n² row and look up to n. 676 is in the row, so the square root of 676 is 26.</p>`}const rules=rows=>`<table class="meas">${rows.map(r=>`<tr><th>${r[0]}</th></tr><tr><td class="formula">${r[1]}</td></tr>`).join("")}</table>`;const rightTri=`<svg viewBox="0 0 220 130" width="220" role="img" aria-label="Right triangle with legs a and b and hypotenuse c" style="display:block;margin:0 auto"><polygon points="20,110 190,110 20,15" fill="var(--sea-soft)" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="3"/><rect x="20" y="96" width="14" height="14" fill="none" stroke="var(--fig-ink)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="105" y="128" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">b</text><text x="8" y="68" text-anchor="middle" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">a</text><text x="116" y="54" font-size="18" font-weight="700" fill="var(--ink)">c</text></svg>`;const FORM={"8.EE.C.8":["Pairs of linear equations",ul(["<strong>Graphing:</strong> the solution is the point where the two lines cross. Read off (x, y).","<strong>Substitution:</strong> when both are solved for y, set the two right sides equal, solve for x, then find y.","<strong>Elimination:</strong> add or subtract the equations so one variable cancels. Multiply an equation first if you need to.","<strong>One solution:</strong> different slopes, so the lines cross once.","<strong>No solution:</strong> same slope, different y-intercepts, so the lines are parallel.","<strong>Infinitely many:</strong> one equation is a multiple of the other, so it is the same line.","Always check your answer in <em>both</em> equations."])],"8.F.A.1":["Functions",ul(["A function is a rule that gives each input exactly one output.","Two different inputs may share an output. One input may not have two outputs.","The graph of a function is the set of ordered pairs (input, output).","Vertical line test: a vertical line touches the graph of a function at most once."])],"8.F.A.3":["Linear functions",`<p class="formula">y = mx + b</p>`+ul(["m is the slope, the change in y for each 1 of x. b is the value when x is 0.","The graph is a straight line, and the change is the same over every equal step.","If the rule squares x, or puts x on the bottom of a fraction, or uses x as an exponent, it is not linear.","To test a table: equal steps in x should give equal changes in y."])],"8.G.B.7":["Pythagorean Theorem",rightTri+`<p class="formula">a<sup>2</sup> + b<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup></p>`+ul(["a and b are the legs. c is the hypotenuse, the longest side, across from the right angle.","To find a leg instead: c<sup>2</sup> − a<sup>2</sup> = b<sup>2</sup>.","On a grid, the legs are the horizontal and vertical distances between the two points.","For a box: length<sup>2</sup> + width<sup>2</sup> + height<sup>2</sup> = diagonal<sup>2</sup>."])],"8.NS.A.1":["Rational and irrational",ul(["<strong>Rational:</strong> can be written as a fraction of two integers. Its decimal either ends or repeats forever.","<strong>Irrational:</strong> cannot. Its decimal never ends and never repeats.","Repeating decimal to a fraction: multiply by 10 for each repeating digit, subtract the original, then divide.","The square root of a whole number that is not a perfect square is irrational."])],"8.EE.A.1":["Integer exponents",ul(["Same base, multiplying: add the exponents.","Same base, dividing: subtract the exponents.","A power raised to a power: multiply the exponents.","A negative exponent means the reciprocal. 3<sup>-3</sup> = 1/3<sup>3</sup>.","Anything to the zero power is 1."])],"8.EE.A.2":["Square roots and cube roots",ul(["x<sup>2</sup> = p has two solutions when p is positive: the positive root and the negative root.","x<sup>3</sup> = p has one solution.","Perfect squares to know: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81, 100, 121, 144, 169, 196, 225.","Perfect cubes to know: 1, 8, 27, 64, 125, 216.","The square root of 2 is irrational."])],"8.EE.B.5":["Slope as a unit rate",ul(["Slope = rise ÷ run between any two points on the line.","For a proportional relationship the slope is the unit rate, and the line passes through (0, 0).","In y = kx the number k is the slope.","To compare a graph with an equation, find the slope of each and compare those."])],"8.G.A.3":["Transformations on coordinates",ul(["<strong>Translation:</strong> add or subtract the same amount from every x, and from every y.","<strong>Reflection across the horizontal axis:</strong> (x, y) becomes (x, -y). <strong>Across the vertical axis:</strong> (x, y) becomes (-x, y).","<strong>Rotation of a quarter turn counterclockwise about the origin:</strong> (x, y) becomes (-y, x). <strong>Of a half turn about the origin:</strong> (x, y) becomes (-x, -y).","<strong>Dilation from the origin by k:</strong> (x, y) becomes (kx, ky). Lengths change by k. Angles do not change."])],"8.SP.A.1":["Scatter plots",ul(["<strong>Positive association:</strong> as one goes up, so does the other. <strong>Negative:</strong> one goes up as the other goes down.","<strong>Linear:</strong> the points lie close to a straight line. <strong>Nonlinear:</strong> they bend.","<strong>Clustering:</strong> points bunched in one area. <strong>Outlier:</strong> a point far from the pattern.","Describe the association, the shape, and any outlier, in that order."])],"F-LE.1":["Linear or exponential",ul(["<strong>Linear:</strong> equal differences over equal intervals. The same amount is added or taken away each step.","<strong>Exponential:</strong> equal ratios over equal intervals. The value is multiplied by the same factor each step.","To test a table: find the differences first, then the ratios. Whichever is constant names the kind."])],"G-SRT.8":["Right triangle trigonometry",rules([["Sine","sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse"],["Cosine","cos θ = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse"],["Tangent","tan θ = opposite ÷ adjacent"]])+ul(["Label the sides from the angle you are working with first.","To find an angle from a ratio, use the inverse key on a calculator.","The Pythagorean theorem still holds and is the quickest check."])]};FORM["A-REI.6"]=FORM["8.EE.C.8"];function formHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>FORM[x]).map(x=>{if(seen.includes(FORM[x][0]))return"";seen.push(FORM[x][0]);return`<h4>${FORM[x][0]}</h4>${FORM[x][1]}`}).join("")}const EX={"8.EE.C.8":[["Solve a pair by substitution",["Pair: y = 5x − 2 and y = x + 10.","Both are solved for y, so set the right sides equal: 5x − 2 = x + 10.","Solve: 4x = 12, so x = 3.","Put x = 3 into either equation: y = 3 + 10 = 13.","Solution (3, 13). Check in the other: 5(3) − 2 = 13."]],["Solve a pair by elimination",["Pair: 3x + y = 14 and x − y = 2.","Add the two equations so y cancels: 4x = 16, so x = 4.","Put x = 4 back in: 4 − y = 2, so y = 2.","Solution (4, 2). Check: 3(4) + 2 = 14."]]],"8.F.A.3":[["Decide whether a table is linear",["Table: x = 0, 1, 2, 3 and y = 5, 9, 13, 17.","x goes up by 1 each step, so check the changes in y: 4, 4, 4.","The changes are equal, so it is linear.","b is the value at x = 0, which is 5, and m is the change, which is 4, so y = 4x + 5."]]],"8.G.B.7":[["Find a missing side",["Legs are 7 and 24. Find the hypotenuse c.","7<sup>2</sup> + 24<sup>2</sup> = c<sup>2</sup>","49 + 576 = 625","c is the square root of 625, which is 25.","To find a leg instead, subtract: c<sup>2</sup> − a<sup>2</sup> = b<sup>2</sup>."]]]};EX["A-REI.6"]=EX["8.EE.C.8"];function exHtml(st){const seen=[];return st.std.filter(x=>EX[x]).map(x=>EX[x].map(e=>{if(seen.includes(e[0]))return"";seen.push(e[0]);return`<h4>${e[0]}</h4><ol class="card-list">${e[1].map(s=>`<li>${s}</li>`).join("")}</ol>`}).join("")).join("")+`<p class="small">These are different problems from the ones on the stop. Use the same steps.</p>`}let gridPts=[],gridJoin=false;const GS=440,GM=10,GU=(GS-68)/(2*GM);function pgridHtml(){const lines=[];if(gridJoin)for(let i=1;i<gridPts.length;i++)lines.push([gridPts[i-1],gridPts[i]]);return`<div class="blk-row"><button class="chip" data-g="join" aria-pressed="${gridJoin}">Connect the points</button><button class="chip" data-g="undo">Undo</button><button class="chip" data-g="clear">Clear</button></div><div class="figure" data-pg style="cursor:crosshair">${F.coordGrid({max:GM,quad4:true,size:GS,points:gridPts,lines})}</div><p class="small">Click the grid to plot a point. Points: ${gridPts.map(p=>`(${p[0]}, ${p[1]})`).join(", ")||"none yet"}</p>`}function pgridBind(b,r){b.querySelectorAll("[data-g]").forEach(x=>x.onclick=()=>{const g=x.dataset.g;if(g==="join")gridJoin=!gridJoin;if(g==="undo")gridPts.pop();if(g==="clear")gridPts=[];r()});const el=b.querySelector("[data-pg] svg");if(!el)return;el.onclick=e=>{const q=el.getBoundingClientRect(),vx=(e.clientX-q.left)*GS/q.width,vy=(e.clientY-q.top)*GS/q.height,x=Math.round((vx-34)/GU)-GM,y=Math.round((GS-34-vy)/GU)-GM;if(Math.abs(x)>GM||Math.abs(y)>GM)return;gridPts.push([x,y]);r()}}const TOOLS={listen:{name:"Computer reads it aloud",std:TEXT_STD,ok:()=>"speechSynthesis"in window,html:listenHtml,bind:listenBind},gloss:{name:"Word help",std:TEXT_STD.concat(WRITE_STD,["SL.8.4"]),html:glossHtml},terms:{name:"Reading terms",std:READ_STD,html:()=>dl(TERMS)},frames:{name:"Answer frames",std:READ_STD,html:framesHtml},gram:{name:"Grammar card",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],html:gramHtml},plan:{name:"Planner",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:planHtml,bind:planBind},sent:{name:"Sentence frames",std:WRITE_STD.concat(["W.8.8","SL.8.4"]),html:sentHtml},check:{name:"Writing checklist",std:WRITE_STD,html:checkHtml},calc:{name:"Calculator",std:MATH_STD,html:calcHtml,bind:calcBind},mult:{name:"Multiplication chart",std:MATH_STD,html:multHtml,bind:(b,r)=>b.querySelectorAll("[data-r]").forEach(c=>c.onclick=()=>{const x=+c.dataset.r,k=+c.dataset.c;multSel=multSel[0]===x&&multSel[1]===k?[0,0]:[x,k];r()})},sq:{name:"Squares chart",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],html:sqHtml},form:{name:"Formula and definition card",std:Object.keys(FORM),html:formHtml},ex:{name:"Worked example",std:Object.keys(EX),html:exHtml},grid:{name:"Coordinate grid",std:["8.EE.C.8","8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","8.G.A.3","8.G.B.7","8.SP.A.1","A-REI.6","F-LE.1"],html:pgridHtml,bind:pgridBind}};const SUP_CAVEATS=[{t:"listen",std:READ_STD,msg:"If the computer read the text aloud, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"passage",msg:"If the text or the table was read to the student, this stop shows listening comprehension, not reading."},{t:"terms",std:READ_STD,msg:"The reading terms card defines cite, inference, connotation, simile, relevant, sufficient, and the difference between a fact and an interpretation, so this stop then shows applying those ideas with a reference."},{t:"frames",msg:"Answer frames give the sentence structure. Rate the evidence and the thinking the student supplied."},{t:"frames",std:["RI.8.9"],msg:"The frames for this stop name the two kinds of disagreement, fact and interpretation, so count the sorting part of the answer as supported and rate the evidence the student found."},{t:"gram",std:["L.8.1","L.9-10.1"],msg:"The grammar card defines verbals, voice, mood, and phrase types with examples, so this stop shows using a reference, not recall."},{t:"plan",msg:"The planner supplies the organization, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"plan",std:["W.8.1","W.9-10.1"],msg:"The planner includes a line for the other side's claim, so count the counterclaim check as supported."},{t:"sent",msg:"Sentence frames supply the linking words and the closing, so count those checks as supported."},{t:"check",msg:"The checklist names what to include. Note that the student used it to revise."},{t:"calc",std:["8.EE.C.8","A-REI.6"],msg:"The calculator does the arithmetic. Rate the setup and the method, not the computation."},{t:"calc",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],msg:"The calculator has a square root key, so rate whether the student knew which squares to add or subtract, not the root itself."},{t:"calc",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.SP.A.1"],msg:"The calculator has power and root keys and does the arithmetic. Rate whether the student chose the right rule and can say what the answer means."},{t:"form",std:["8.NS.A.1","8.EE.A.1","8.EE.A.2","8.EE.B.5","8.G.A.3","8.SP.A.1"],msg:"The card gives the exponent rules, the transformation rules, and the words for describing a scatter plot, so this stop shows applying a stated rule rather than recall."},{t:"grid",std:["8.G.A.3"],msg:"If the grid tool was used to move the corners one square at a time, note that the student read the new coordinates off a picture rather than working them out from the rule."},{t:"plan",std:["W.8.2"],msg:"The planner supplies the order of the explanation, so count the organization check as supported, not independent."},{t:"calc",std:["8.F.A.1","8.F.A.3","F-LE.1"],msg:"The calculator worked out the values. Rate whether the student knew what to compute and what the result shows."},{t:"sq",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],msg:"The squares chart gives the square roots, so count the final step of each question as supported and rate the setup."},{t:"form",std:["8.EE.C.8","A-REI.6"],msg:"The card lists the methods and the three cases for the number of solutions, so count the “how many solutions” question as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.F.A.1"],msg:"The card states the definition of a function and the vertical line test, so this stop then shows applying a stated rule rather than recalling it."},{t:"form",std:["8.F.A.3","F-LE.1"],msg:"The card says how to test a table and which forms are not linear, so count the sorting questions as supported."},{t:"form",std:["8.G.B.7","G-SRT.8"],msg:"The card gives the theorem, the rule for a leg, the three-dimensional version, and the trigonometric ratios, so this stop shows applying them with a reference."},{t:"ex",msg:"A worked example models the method. Count the matching questions as supported."},{t:"grid",std:["8.EE.C.8","A-REI.6"],msg:"If the pair of equations was solved by plotting on the grid tool, note that the student solved by graphing rather than by algebra."},{t:"grid",std:["8.G.B.7"],msg:"If the grid tool was used to count the horizontal and vertical legs, note that the student read them off a picture rather than subtracting the coordinates."},{t:"scribe",msg:"If an adult wrote, rate the ideas and the organization. Do not rate conventions the adult supplied."},{t:"fewer",msg:"If questions were skipped, change the “of” number in the score to the number the student tried."},{t:"hint",msg:"Note which question got the hint, and count that question as supported."}];return{stops:STOPS,levelups:LEVELUPS,tools:TOOLS,supCaveats:SUP_CAVEATS,logNames:LOG_NAMES,stopLogs,onToolChange:()=>stopSpeak()}}});
